WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:16.630
Thanks for joining today.

00:00:16.630 --> 00:00:20.700
This is going to be another private live Q&amp;A.

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:24.790
And I'll do my best to answer questions in
two different forms.

00:00:24.790 --> 00:00:32.080
We will alternate between questions that were
submitted ahead of time and questions in the

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:33.810
live feed.

00:00:33.810 --> 00:00:37.960
I'm going to start with some of the questions
that were submitted beforehand and then people

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:40.280
can add questions to the chat.

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:42.629
The more specific, the better.

00:00:42.629 --> 00:00:46.530
The broader they are, the harder it will be
for me to answer.

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:52.579
If they're really long, I will probably also
skip them so try to keep things very concise.

00:00:52.579 --> 00:01:04.380
Let me just take a look at a few of the questions
here that are marketing and PR slash launch focused. It'll be fun for me to stretch these muscles since I haven't

00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:07.180
done anything specific to these type of topics
in quite a long time.

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:08.880
So here it goes.

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:12.640
The first question that I have in front of
me is from Jon.

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:14.840
I'm going to keep last names out of it, but
J-O-N.

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:19.149
"If you were going to launch a new fast casual
restaurant, how would you gather the crowd

00:01:19.149 --> 00:01:23.909
before building the restaurant, i.e., making
sure people would come when it opens?"

00:01:23.909 --> 00:01:32.100
Well, the theme that I think is going to recur
in many of my answers is one of risk mitigation.

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:37.030
Perhaps I'm known by some or thought of by
some as a risk taker because I do things like

00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:40.980
invest in early state startups, which tend
to go to zero.

00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:46.140
I would say a very high percentage, maybe
50 to 70 percent of my investments, go to

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:47.520
zero.

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:52.290
But you have to keep in mind a number of other
factors, and this relates to poker and other

00:01:52.290 --> 00:02:00.960
games of skill that are blended with a high
degree of chance and part of that is, say,

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:07.490
bankroll, bet sizing, knowing your maximum
downside compared to your maximum upside.

00:02:07.490 --> 00:02:15.850
That's just a frame perspective that I would
like to present as one, I look through for

00:02:15.850 --> 00:02:20.370
evaluating almost any type of question like
this, any type of opportunity, any type of

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:23.360
risk.

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:27.000
Coming back to fast casual restaurant, what
would I do?

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:29.489
How would I gather the crowd before building
the restaurant?

00:02:29.489 --> 00:02:37.740
I would look at low risk, capped downside
ways of experimenting with this concept.

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:44.220
For instance, in Austin, Texas where my home
base is, you see many successful restaurants

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:46.470
begin as food trucks.

00:02:46.470 --> 00:02:47.470
Why?

00:02:47.470 --> 00:02:53.989
Because the build out, of course, is much
less, the regulations surrounding food trucks

00:02:53.989 --> 00:03:02.769
in some respects far less onerous than those
of restaurants, and you could potentially

00:03:02.769 --> 00:03:05.459
prototype taking that type of approach.

00:03:05.459 --> 00:03:10.230
A even lower cost approach could be having
a popup in another restaurant.

00:03:10.230 --> 00:03:14.790
When I lived in San Francisco, there were
a number of restaurants that were later successful

00:03:14.790 --> 00:03:16.739
that began as popups.

00:03:16.739 --> 00:03:22.829
Say Thursday nights, or probably an off night,
so maybe a Monday or a Tuesday night in a

00:03:22.829 --> 00:03:28.799
given retail location where they are effectively
renting the infrastructure of a preexisting

00:03:28.799 --> 00:03:29.799
restaurant.

00:03:29.799 --> 00:03:35.949
Now, I'll leave it to you and your lawyers
to determine the legality of that, but nonetheless,

00:03:35.949 --> 00:03:39.859
many successful restaurants I'm aware of in
San Francisco began that way.

00:03:39.859 --> 00:03:43.919
And it was important they began that way because
they never got it right immediately out of

00:03:43.919 --> 00:03:44.919
the gate.

00:03:44.919 --> 00:03:50.730
I think it would be a big mistake to just
YOLO all of your chips in at once and try

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:56.449
this concept hoping that the crowds will get
there, or assuming the crowds will congregate

00:03:56.449 --> 00:04:01.389
if you haven't really kicked the tires, so
another option would be a popup in another

00:04:01.389 --> 00:04:02.389
restaurant.

00:04:02.389 --> 00:04:03.419
There's a book.

00:04:03.419 --> 00:04:08.980
It's out of date, it is out of print, probably,
called Guerilla Financing, that may also give

00:04:08.980 --> 00:04:12.639
you some ideas for how to bootstrap in different
capacities.

00:04:12.639 --> 00:04:17.519
I found that book very helpful when I was
bootstrapping one of my first companies.

00:04:17.519 --> 00:04:24.190
But the point here is really to identify the
risk factors and this relates to an exercise

00:04:24.190 --> 00:04:27.949
that I do at least once a quarter, many of
you have heard of it, called fear-setting.

00:04:27.949 --> 00:04:33.650
That was the topic of my last TED Talk, which
has, I think, 10 million views or so now.

00:04:33.650 --> 00:04:39.270
Identifying the risk factors and going down
the list, risk by risk, and trying to identify

00:04:39.270 --> 00:04:44.090
how you can either eliminate those or mitigate
the likelihood of them occurring.

00:04:44.090 --> 00:04:52.520
For instance, if you had a popup and you wanted
to try to draw some type of crowd, and really

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:59.319
your goal is iteration and improvement of
your concept, of your menu, of the experience.

00:04:59.319 --> 00:05:05.530
You could partner potentially with, say, organizations
or groups who would stand to benefit from

00:05:05.530 --> 00:05:06.530
a rev share.

00:05:06.530 --> 00:05:07.530
I'm making this up.

00:05:07.530 --> 00:05:09.520
I don't know exactly what fast casual refers
to here.

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:11.220
Let's say it's fast healthy.

00:05:11.220 --> 00:05:17.969
You could approach local CrossFit gyms or
gyms of some type and say, "Hey, for every

00:05:17.969 --> 00:05:20.539
person you send to this restaurant, I will
give you..."

00:05:20.539 --> 00:05:22.000
What do you care?

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:23.310
100 percent of the profit.

00:05:23.310 --> 00:05:24.909
You don't have to give them 10 percent.

00:05:24.909 --> 00:05:28.490
You don't actually need to make a profit as
long as you break even.

00:05:28.490 --> 00:05:32.790
Beyond covering cost, you could just say,
"I will give you all of the revenue that comes

00:05:32.790 --> 00:05:35.250
from any of your members you send to this
restaurant."

00:05:35.250 --> 00:05:39.810
Because, again, what you're trying to gather
is feedback first and foremost so that you

00:05:39.810 --> 00:05:46.879
can improve before you have more sunk cost
in some type of, say, food truck or retail

00:05:46.879 --> 00:05:47.879
location.

00:05:47.879 --> 00:05:48.879
All right, let me take one more here and then
I'll jump into the questions in the chat and

00:05:48.879 --> 00:05:49.879
we'll see where it goes.

00:05:49.879 --> 00:05:50.879
Here we go.

00:05:50.879 --> 00:05:51.879
Here's a question from Matt.

00:05:51.879 --> 00:05:53.270
"What would be your approach if you wanted
to raise awareness of a new productivity tool?

00:05:53.270 --> 00:05:56.889
It's genuinely different from any of the approaches
taken so far and helps people apply the great

00:05:56.889 --> 00:05:58.750
advice they know, but don't follow.

00:05:58.750 --> 00:06:00.789
Easy to pick up and use just minutes a day.

00:06:00.789 --> 00:06:05.479
There's free versions, but I have no list
or platform to gain initial traction.

00:06:05.479 --> 00:06:07.910
Share to bloggers, podcasts, et cetera in
the space.

00:06:07.910 --> 00:06:08.910
Any advice?

00:06:08.910 --> 00:06:11.020
Welcome and thanks for the counsel and company
over the years."

00:06:11.020 --> 00:06:19.400
I have a few recommendations here and the
first is really observing people, ideally,

00:06:19.400 --> 00:06:21.150
not just asking people.

00:06:21.150 --> 00:06:23.440
I'll just draw a contrast here.

00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:28.090
If you think you have a great idea for a business,
it could be a product based business or service

00:06:28.090 --> 00:06:31.379
based business, and you ask your friends,
"Would you buy this?

00:06:31.379 --> 00:06:34.759
Would you pay X amount for such and such a
thing?"

00:06:34.759 --> 00:06:40.280
In most cases, we will have some friends,
maybe most of our friends will say, "Yes,

00:06:40.280 --> 00:06:42.169
I would absolutely do that."

00:06:42.169 --> 00:06:46.680
However, their behavior changes a bit when
you then put out your hand and say, "Fantastic,

00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:49.300
I'm taking pre-orders, I'll give you a 20
percent discount.

00:06:49.300 --> 00:06:51.570
Could I take that payment now?"

00:06:51.570 --> 00:06:56.400
The point is, asking for opinions and getting
people to take action are two very, very different

00:06:56.400 --> 00:06:57.400
things.

00:06:57.400 --> 00:07:01.819
And likewise, observing people using a tool
is very different from asking them how they

00:07:01.819 --> 00:07:03.039
might use a tool.

00:07:03.039 --> 00:07:07.349
What I would suggest in this case is before
you even think about launching, make sure

00:07:07.349 --> 00:07:11.229
you're solving a problem that people want
solved.

00:07:11.229 --> 00:07:16.659
If something has never been done before, I
find this very important to put under scrutiny

00:07:16.659 --> 00:07:19.259
and really pay attention to.

00:07:19.259 --> 00:07:21.860
One of my first steps might be paying 20 people
to use it.

00:07:21.860 --> 00:07:22.979
That might sound funny.

00:07:22.979 --> 00:07:25.340
If there are free options available, why would
I pay someone?

00:07:25.340 --> 00:07:27.289
You're paying someone for their time and feedback.

00:07:27.289 --> 00:07:33.159
This will sound very similar to my first answer,
and you could pay 20 people to use it and

00:07:33.159 --> 00:07:38.319
observe exactly the flow through the product,
exactly their feedback, encourage them to

00:07:38.319 --> 00:07:39.599
speak out loud.

00:07:39.599 --> 00:07:42.169
There are services that provide for this.

00:07:42.169 --> 00:07:43.919
Usertesting.com would be one example.

00:07:43.919 --> 00:07:47.060
I haven't used it personally, but I know startups
who have.

00:07:47.060 --> 00:07:52.689
May be beyond your price range, but you could
also take lower tech options or off the shelf

00:07:52.689 --> 00:07:53.689
options.

00:07:53.689 --> 00:07:57.969
You could have people record themselves using
something like Loom, which is a tool I use

00:07:57.969 --> 00:08:05.319
a lot for screen capture for training employees
and contractors, feedback on various designs.

00:08:05.319 --> 00:08:11.419
You could have them simply capture their experience
on, say, laptop with something like Loom,

00:08:11.419 --> 00:08:12.569
and then share those with you.

00:08:12.569 --> 00:08:18.949
And you could find these people to test your
product on Craigslist or really just about

00:08:18.949 --> 00:08:22.309
anywhere, or you could do this in person.

00:08:22.309 --> 00:08:28.590
I know a number of well-known CEOs who began
their companies that now have 1,000, 2,000,

00:08:28.590 --> 00:08:35.400
5,000 employees by recording user testing
sessions in person with video.

00:08:35.400 --> 00:08:38.770
You could simply have them in front of you
at their laptop, encourage them to speak out

00:08:38.770 --> 00:08:39.770
loud.

00:08:39.770 --> 00:08:43.890
Then, you have an iPhone or something and
you're simply recording the screen as they

00:08:43.890 --> 00:08:47.070
walk through it so that you can correlate
things properly.

00:08:47.070 --> 00:08:51.890
But I would say begin with more intelligence
gathering.

00:08:51.890 --> 00:08:59.110
That's where I would start, and then in terms
of how to get the word out, sending free software

00:08:59.110 --> 00:09:04.690
to bloggers, podcasts that are in the space
can't hurt, but you're going to need to have

00:09:04.690 --> 00:09:06.600
a good pitch.

00:09:06.600 --> 00:09:13.639
I get sent, or I should say people tend to
send me thousands of different pitches per

00:09:13.639 --> 00:09:15.490
day, literally thousands.

00:09:15.490 --> 00:09:20.140
The vast majority I simply deflect because
I don't have the bandwidth, nor does my team

00:09:20.140 --> 00:09:24.370
have the bandwidth to even digest or read
these pitches.

00:09:24.370 --> 00:09:26.149
I would think of other approaches.

00:09:26.149 --> 00:09:32.459
The way that I have tackled this with each
book launch, as an example, is looking for

00:09:32.459 --> 00:09:38.820
the neglected but powerful channel and then
also the nascent, up-and-coming channel that

00:09:38.820 --> 00:09:41.660
is ramping in influence.

00:09:41.660 --> 00:09:46.570
For the first book, The 4-Hour Workweek, that
was bloggers, for instance.

00:09:46.570 --> 00:09:48.949
Most people were like, "Blogs, what the hell
is a blog?" in 2005.

00:09:48.949 --> 00:09:53.339
But at the time, I knew that they were pulling
more weight for book sales in some respects

00:09:53.339 --> 00:09:58.770
than the darlings of network television and
syndicated radio at the time so I paid a lot

00:09:58.770 --> 00:09:59.770
of attention to that.

00:09:59.770 --> 00:10:02.080
For The 4-Hour Chef, it was podcasts.

00:10:02.080 --> 00:10:07.950
This was in 2012, after which I launched my
own podcast in 2014.

00:10:07.950 --> 00:10:11.430
I would also look at the landscape of tools.

00:10:11.430 --> 00:10:16.529
And another question I would ask is, which
of these tools or platforms are you actually

00:10:16.529 --> 00:10:18.680
going to engage in for the long term?

00:10:18.680 --> 00:10:25.110
You don't have to decide that upfront, but
where do your natural skills and enthusiasm

00:10:25.110 --> 00:10:27.090
overlap such that you will have staying power?

00:10:27.090 --> 00:10:30.631
If you're not going to have staying power,
chances are, beyond a flash in a pan, it's

00:10:30.631 --> 00:10:31.880
not going to be helpful.

00:10:31.880 --> 00:10:41.060
You can still use one-time-only PR approaches,
much like I really targeted Digg.com, D-I-G-G.com,

00:10:41.060 --> 00:10:44.490
founded by my friend Kevin Rose way back in
the day to try to get on the homepage for

00:10:44.490 --> 00:10:51.009
launching The 4-Hour Body because I understood
the power of Digg at that time for acting

00:10:51.009 --> 00:11:00.399
as a front-page shopping list, a tastemaking
service of sorts, much like landing at the

00:11:00.399 --> 00:11:04.320
top of Hacker News or something like that
today.

00:11:04.320 --> 00:11:12.410
Those are a few thoughts, and I will come
back to some related themes in the next question

00:11:12.410 --> 00:11:15.520
and then I'm going to go to some in the live
Q&amp;A, so don't worry, guys.

00:11:15.520 --> 00:11:16.600
Here's the third one.

00:11:16.600 --> 00:11:21.360
This is from Josh, and I'm just going down
in order that I have them in front of me.

00:11:21.360 --> 00:11:24.120
This is a question related to advising tech
companies.

00:11:24.120 --> 00:11:29.620
"When you were advising tech companies, what
were your most successful marketing campaigns/contribution

00:11:29.620 --> 00:11:30.680
to the companies?

00:11:30.680 --> 00:11:34.660
Any notable misses you're willing to share?"

00:11:34.660 --> 00:11:42.410
For those who have not seen the range of companies
that I've worked with, you can go to angel.co/Tim.

00:11:42.410 --> 00:11:45.310
You can see quite a handful there.

00:11:45.310 --> 00:11:53.420
In terms of most successful marketing campaign/contributions,
contributions I'm going to focus on because

00:11:53.420 --> 00:11:59.769
oftentimes where I have aimed to help has
been unrelated to anything immediately public

00:11:59.769 --> 00:12:00.769
facing.

00:12:00.769 --> 00:12:07.860
For instance, for TaskRabbit, way in the early
days, I helped Leah raise her first round

00:12:07.860 --> 00:12:15.790
of financing when the company was actually
completely named differently based, I believe,

00:12:15.790 --> 00:12:20.340
just in Cambridge or in the Boston area, and
we met in the Bay area.

00:12:20.340 --> 00:12:23.730
I helped her to syndicate her financing.

00:12:23.730 --> 00:12:28.810
I introduced her to lead investors who ended
up helping her raise money and then much later

00:12:28.810 --> 00:12:31.870
that company was sold to IKEA.

00:12:31.870 --> 00:12:41.170
In other cases, it could be related to helping
companies apply needed leverage or user pressure

00:12:41.170 --> 00:12:47.190
for, say, regulatory purposes.

00:12:47.190 --> 00:12:55.699
I was very active in this capacity with Uber,
particularly with some precedent-setting examples

00:12:55.699 --> 00:13:00.149
where situations were very, very high stakes
in places like Washington, DC.

00:13:00.149 --> 00:13:09.389
With my platform and via other types of back
channel work, that's a separate category of

00:13:09.389 --> 00:13:11.329
help that I sometimes provide.

00:13:11.329 --> 00:13:18.420
In terms of marketing, I think the most notable
example is probably — and there are many,

00:13:18.420 --> 00:13:23.319
many, many examples, but the most notable,
well-known example is probably the build to

00:13:23.319 --> 00:13:31.410
business competition that I helped to design
with Tobi and Harley of Shopify.

00:13:31.410 --> 00:13:34.880
If you want to read more about that, there's
a fair amount out there.

00:13:34.880 --> 00:13:41.600
There is a piece in Fortune magazine called
"The Invisible Selling Machine" about Shopify.

00:13:41.600 --> 00:13:46.449
This was a huge piece in the magazine some
time ago, and it goes into quite a bit of

00:13:46.449 --> 00:13:51.880
detail about my involvement in the build a
business competition, and then overall what

00:13:51.880 --> 00:13:53.350
that looked like.

00:13:53.350 --> 00:13:59.230
That was very, very, very high ROI, high leverage
for Shopify.

00:13:59.230 --> 00:14:05.100
And if you don't have a Fortune subscription,
you can go to the Web Archive, I think it's

00:14:05.100 --> 00:14:09.269
just web.archive.org, otherwise known as The
Wayback Machine.

00:14:09.269 --> 00:14:11.589
You can grab a snapshot and take a look at
that article.

00:14:11.589 --> 00:14:15.020
All right, let's hop into the live feed and
see what we got here.

00:14:15.020 --> 00:14:16.020
All right, we got a lot of questions.

00:14:16.020 --> 00:14:17.020
I'm going to do my best to get to some of
them.

00:14:17.020 --> 00:14:18.020
All right, bear with me a minute.

00:14:18.020 --> 00:14:19.020
Okay, we've got a lot of good questions.

00:14:19.020 --> 00:14:20.020
All right, well, I'll take a self-interested
one first.

00:14:20.020 --> 00:14:21.020
This is from Joe Chen.

00:14:21.020 --> 00:14:23.370
"What is your biggest project/challenge at
the moment and how can we support you?"

00:14:23.370 --> 00:14:32.990
I do have a very absurd and I think hilarious
and fun art project coming out in the not

00:14:32.990 --> 00:14:35.480
too distant future, so keep an eye out for
that.

00:14:35.480 --> 00:14:44.190
At the very least, just taking a break to
laugh at the whole thing when it comes out.

00:14:44.190 --> 00:14:48.800
That's how you could support, but I'll keep
moving here.

00:14:48.800 --> 00:14:51.510
All right, this one is from Scott.

00:14:51.510 --> 00:14:56.040
"What's a takeaway or lesson you wish more
of us listeners paid attention to/followed

00:14:56.040 --> 00:14:57.420
through with?"

00:14:57.420 --> 00:15:02.860
I would say in this day and age, probably
the low-information diet as described in The

00:15:02.860 --> 00:15:05.070
4-Hour Workweek and here's my phone here.

00:15:05.070 --> 00:15:13.050
I have not had any social apps on this phone
in two years, and that's not because I broadcast

00:15:13.050 --> 00:15:16.440
that for sitting on my moral high horse.

00:15:16.440 --> 00:15:25.519
It's because I understand that it is playing
a dangerous game to assume that you can use

00:15:25.519 --> 00:15:28.110
willpower and self-control.

00:15:28.110 --> 00:15:33.930
At the very least, it is exhausting and you
can save your mental calories for something

00:15:33.930 --> 00:15:35.720
more important.

00:15:35.720 --> 00:15:44.860
To have this type of addictive technology
embedded into your phone with notifications

00:15:44.860 --> 00:15:55.399
that you're subject to 24/7, through which
algorithms are being wielded against you that

00:15:55.399 --> 00:16:03.779
have billions of dollars in teams of data
scientists and UI experts behind them, I think

00:16:03.779 --> 00:16:09.550
it's unnecessarily challenging to subject
yourself to that and to expect to be able

00:16:09.550 --> 00:16:15.199
to overtake that and single task effectively.

00:16:15.199 --> 00:16:19.009
And some people say, "Well, I have better
self-control.

00:16:19.009 --> 00:16:20.240
You can just use self-control."

00:16:20.240 --> 00:16:27.270
That may be true, but I could also have junk
food littered all over my house, and I can

00:16:27.270 --> 00:16:28.270
make the same argument.

00:16:28.270 --> 00:16:32.740
I could say, "Well, if I have enough self-control,
I won't eat the junk food," but it's a hell

00:16:32.740 --> 00:16:36.300
of a lot easier just to remove all the junk
food from your house.

00:16:36.300 --> 00:16:40.649
I do think the low-information diet, this
applies not just a social media, but certainly

00:16:40.649 --> 00:16:49.610
any type of push news, which could just be
reactively picking up the newspaper every

00:16:49.610 --> 00:16:52.829
day when you go to grab a cup of coffee at
a gas station or something like that.

00:16:52.829 --> 00:17:01.450
It doesn't need to be digital, but the low-information
diet, I think is very much underestimated

00:17:01.450 --> 00:17:03.779
in its power.

00:17:03.779 --> 00:17:11.470
If you can single task for a few hours a day
without any interruption, which is why I use

00:17:11.470 --> 00:17:17.310
Do Not Disturb and so on my laptop as well
as frequently as I do, and my phone is almost

00:17:17.310 --> 00:17:25.250
always on airplane mode, you will have a huge
competitive advantage over the majority of

00:17:25.250 --> 00:17:30.820
people who are — let's just call them knowledge
workers, anyone who works on a computer.

00:17:30.820 --> 00:17:34.830
If you have the ability to single task for,
let's just call it, three hours a day in a

00:17:34.830 --> 00:17:40.570
single block of time, you have an unbelievable
competitive advantage.

00:17:40.570 --> 00:17:41.810
I would say the low-information diet.

00:17:41.810 --> 00:17:42.810
All right, let's see what else we have here.

00:17:42.810 --> 00:17:43.810
Always takes me a little while to read these
things, so thanks for the patience.

00:17:43.810 --> 00:17:44.810
Okay.

00:17:44.810 --> 00:17:45.810
Okay.

00:17:45.810 --> 00:17:46.810
This is a question from Renalyn.

00:17:46.810 --> 00:17:48.210
"What are your thoughts on Substack as more
of a pull way to get information?"

00:17:48.210 --> 00:17:58.050
Yeah, I think that opting into highly curated
sources of information that are either relevant

00:17:58.050 --> 00:18:06.600
to you in the near term or actionable to you
in the near term is far superior to being

00:18:06.600 --> 00:18:16.270
lost in the waves and chop of online assault
that is uncontrolled, if we're talking about

00:18:16.270 --> 00:18:17.270
information.

00:18:17.270 --> 00:18:20.620
I think newsletters certainly are far superior.

00:18:20.620 --> 00:18:25.160
You can still use them to procrastinate in
the same way that you can use reading books

00:18:25.160 --> 00:18:30.040
as a socially acceptable way to procrastinate,
so just be aware of that.

00:18:30.040 --> 00:18:36.420
As Kathy Sierra once phrased it to me, "Focus
on just-in-time information, not just-in-case

00:18:36.420 --> 00:18:37.420
information."

00:18:37.420 --> 00:18:42.650
If you think you might need to act on say,
the contents of a book six to 12 months from

00:18:42.650 --> 00:18:46.500
now, it doesn't really do you a lot of good
to read it now is my general feeling.

00:18:46.500 --> 00:18:51.380
I would read it closer to the point at which
you need to actually implement it.

00:18:51.380 --> 00:18:56.940
But I would say that something like Substack
or newsletters in general I think are superior

00:18:56.940 --> 00:19:05.060
in so much as they force you to make decisions
about high-priority/low-priority information

00:19:05.060 --> 00:19:09.990
ahead of time so that you're not making all
those decisions on the fly.

00:19:09.990 --> 00:19:17.081
Because, look, we're all simple creatures
and the algorithms will beat you, whether

00:19:17.081 --> 00:19:22.970
it's just showing a picture of Kim Kardashian
ass or some celebrity or who the hell knows?

00:19:22.970 --> 00:19:24.160
It's going to pull you off task.

00:19:24.160 --> 00:19:30.030
I don't care who you are, so just keep the
junk food out of your house, metaphorically

00:19:30.030 --> 00:19:31.030
speaking.

00:19:31.030 --> 00:19:32.420
Let's see.

00:19:32.420 --> 00:19:38.600
Comment on Cal Newport and great books on
deep, deep learning, deep work.

00:19:38.600 --> 00:19:39.970
I agree with that.

00:19:39.970 --> 00:19:40.970
Let's see.

00:19:40.970 --> 00:19:44.450
Ila, question, "One of the most enjoyable
parts of your show is listening to your personal

00:19:44.450 --> 00:19:47.300
journey and your interviewees' journeys of
transcendence.

00:19:47.300 --> 00:19:50.000
Any recent spiritual shifts that you would
not mind sharing?"

00:19:50.000 --> 00:19:51.050
I don't mind sharing.

00:19:51.050 --> 00:19:59.540
I was actually recently in the mountains of
New Mexico and experienced some of the most

00:19:59.540 --> 00:20:08.260
profound and extreme time dilation I've ever
felt, certainly in a sober state with a friend

00:20:08.260 --> 00:20:09.260
of mine.

00:20:09.260 --> 00:20:16.810
And so we were hiking at altitude on and off
for several days and by, say, day three, thinking

00:20:16.810 --> 00:20:23.310
back to the morning of even day two or day
one felt like looking back to three weeks

00:20:23.310 --> 00:20:25.070
or four weeks prior.

00:20:25.070 --> 00:20:31.630
Being in nature, I think partially contributing
to that was the fact that we changed locations

00:20:31.630 --> 00:20:35.410
so many times and that the topography of the
land changed so many times.

00:20:35.410 --> 00:20:41.250
It could as well have been four or five different
countries we visited given how different the

00:20:41.250 --> 00:20:44.560
landscapes were and the wildlife and so on.

00:20:44.560 --> 00:20:50.760
But that time dilation I think highlights
something for me at least, which is there

00:20:50.760 --> 00:20:58.090
are different ways to extend or, better said,
I think, expand life, to live in an expansive

00:20:58.090 --> 00:21:00.290
state more often.

00:21:00.290 --> 00:21:09.280
And many of the, let's just call them post-economic,
extremely wealthy, successful in financial

00:21:09.280 --> 00:21:14.430
terms, founders and CEOs and so on, I know
want to extend health span, which I think

00:21:14.430 --> 00:21:21.640
is a very worthwhile objective, looking at
science and technology for extending healthy

00:21:21.640 --> 00:21:23.570
years.

00:21:23.570 --> 00:21:29.910
However, you can live a long time, but almost
never be present.

00:21:29.910 --> 00:21:34.130
I'm not saying that's true for these people,
but I know people who live a long time and

00:21:34.130 --> 00:21:39.030
they're so preoccupied about the past or the
future or the news or the politician on TV

00:21:39.030 --> 00:21:45.150
or whatever, that they have a lot of time
contraction.

00:21:45.150 --> 00:21:47.810
Time passes very quickly for them.

00:21:47.810 --> 00:21:56.890
What if you could design a life and pre-schedule
experiences such that you have the type of

00:21:56.890 --> 00:22:02.750
time dilation I'm talking about, where let's
just say even twice a year, you have two trips

00:22:02.750 --> 00:22:08.110
each a week long, and each of those one week
trips actually feels like three months?

00:22:08.110 --> 00:22:15.460
Well, have you then in that case, effectively
added — let's just say each one feels like

00:22:15.460 --> 00:22:16.460
three months.

00:22:16.460 --> 00:22:21.800
Okay, have you added six months to each year
by doing that in terms of your experiential

00:22:21.800 --> 00:22:23.130
lifespan?

00:22:23.130 --> 00:22:25.040
If so, that's very interesting, right?

00:22:25.040 --> 00:22:29.860
Now you're living, let's just call it 50 percent
longer in your remaining years.

00:22:29.860 --> 00:22:35.940
Does that have the net/net positive or even
a greater benefit than simply extending your

00:22:35.940 --> 00:22:40.050
biological lifespan by that period of time?

00:22:40.050 --> 00:22:41.080
I don't know.

00:22:41.080 --> 00:22:47.740
I do know that's a question that's been on
my mind and we can certainly pursue both,

00:22:47.740 --> 00:22:54.610
but one is available here and now, and the
others are more so a scientific roadmap with

00:22:54.610 --> 00:22:56.210
possibilities.

00:22:56.210 --> 00:23:03.870
I think this type of time dilation through
very deliberately designed experience and

00:23:03.870 --> 00:23:09.550
immersion is very seductive and rewarding.

00:23:09.550 --> 00:23:13.210
All right, let's come back.

00:23:13.210 --> 00:23:14.210
I think it's Kale.

00:23:14.210 --> 00:23:15.530
It might be Kali, I apologize.

00:23:15.530 --> 00:23:17.270
I don't know how to pronounce it.

00:23:17.270 --> 00:23:18.270
"Love your work, Tim.

00:23:18.270 --> 00:23:20.640
Caught up with Derek Sivers in New Zealand
a couple weekends ago.

00:23:20.640 --> 00:23:21.740
He told me he missed you."

00:23:21.740 --> 00:23:23.920
Well, I miss Derek too.

00:23:23.920 --> 00:23:27.920
He's a great guy and has been on the podcast
several times.

00:23:27.920 --> 00:23:32.080
If people haven't heard Derek Sivers on the
podcast, they should and I do need to catch

00:23:32.080 --> 00:23:33.080
up with him.

00:23:33.080 --> 00:23:34.080
Let me come back to some of the questions
that were pre-submitted, answer a handful

00:23:34.080 --> 00:23:35.080
of those, and then I'll come back to questions
in the chat.

00:23:35.080 --> 00:23:36.080
Feel free to drop them in and I will continue
to answer them.

00:23:36.080 --> 00:23:37.080
Let me get back to some of these pre-submitted
questions.

00:23:37.080 --> 00:23:38.080
All right, here we go.

00:23:38.080 --> 00:23:39.080
This is from Ben.

00:23:39.080 --> 00:23:40.080
"We have invented a way, patent pending, to
add a second story to a house without the

00:23:40.080 --> 00:23:41.080
owners moving out.

00:23:41.080 --> 00:23:42.130
This product and process is revolutionary
for so many people.

00:23:42.130 --> 00:23:45.660
However, we keep going back and forth about
whether to create a franchise model or just

00:23:45.660 --> 00:23:48.770
to sell the proprietary system that allows
you to do it.

00:23:48.770 --> 00:23:51.390
The catch is that it definitely requires training
to use it properly.

00:23:51.390 --> 00:23:55.330
Love to get your feedback about how you would
launch it.

00:23:55.330 --> 00:23:57.370
Very grateful for you and the value you have
brought to my life."

00:23:57.370 --> 00:23:58.770
Well, thank you, Ben.

00:23:58.770 --> 00:24:01.240
Here are my first thoughts.

00:24:01.240 --> 00:24:07.300
Construction, adding stories, et cetera, does
not sound, at least immediately to me, as

00:24:07.300 --> 00:24:10.280
a liability-free endeavor.

00:24:10.280 --> 00:24:18.610
I assume there are some risks involved, as
you mentioned, and I think implied that it

00:24:18.610 --> 00:24:25.790
definitely requires training to use it properly
and using it properly seems important.

00:24:25.790 --> 00:24:31.740
Looking at this again through the lens of
minimizing or capping your downside risk,

00:24:31.740 --> 00:24:39.000
I would say a lot of folks think about business
risk in terms of profit and loss.

00:24:39.000 --> 00:24:41.730
How much does it cost me to make X?

00:24:41.730 --> 00:24:44.010
How much can I sell X for?

00:24:44.010 --> 00:24:45.440
What is my margin?

00:24:45.440 --> 00:24:49.950
Can I cover my various costs, maybe employees,
et cetera?

00:24:49.950 --> 00:24:53.590
But there are other factors that are often
neglected.

00:24:53.590 --> 00:24:59.340
In this particular case, there are questions
around legal cost and legal liability.

00:24:59.340 --> 00:25:05.380
I know that developing and rolling out a franchise
properly can be very capital intensive if

00:25:05.380 --> 00:25:09.650
you're the franchisor, and I wouldn't underestimate
that.

00:25:09.650 --> 00:25:15.190
Step number one, I think, in evaluating a
number of these would be running some models

00:25:15.190 --> 00:25:23.610
and putting together just basic mathematics
based on conversations with competent legal

00:25:23.610 --> 00:25:27.330
counsel, and there are people who specialize,
of course, in both of these.

00:25:27.330 --> 00:25:35.680
There is also a great book by Stephen Key
called One Simple Idea: Turn Your Dreams into

00:25:35.680 --> 00:25:38.500
a Licensing Goldmine While Letting Others
Do the Work.

00:25:38.500 --> 00:25:39.500
That's a great subtitle.

00:25:39.500 --> 00:25:42.920
I didn't realize that was a subtitle, but
One Simple Idea by Stephen Key.

00:25:42.920 --> 00:25:50.260
He is an exceptionally successful inventor
who generally does not venture, meaning he

00:25:50.260 --> 00:25:55.070
doesn't generally manufacture and distribute
and sell product himself.

00:25:55.070 --> 00:26:00.890
He's coming up with ideas, often with very
inexpensive prototyping, and then licensing

00:26:00.890 --> 00:26:06.530
those ideas to other companies in most cases.

00:26:06.530 --> 00:26:13.240
That book I think is worth a read, not because
it is immediately comparable since he's mostly

00:26:13.240 --> 00:26:19.510
operating in the world of toys, but I do think
it will raise questions that are valuable

00:26:19.510 --> 00:26:21.260
for you.

00:26:21.260 --> 00:26:23.880
Another one to look at is Built to Sell.

00:26:23.880 --> 00:26:26.070
This is a book by John Warrillow.

00:26:26.070 --> 00:26:33.140
If you were ever considering the possibility
of being able to sell this company, and that,

00:26:33.140 --> 00:26:38.970
I think, is worth looking at, even if you've
never considered that possibility, because

00:26:38.970 --> 00:26:45.450
it will help you to identify where you as
a proprietor, as a business owner, could end

00:26:45.450 --> 00:26:50.120
up being a bottleneck, whether it's for scaling
the company or ultimately for not just scaling

00:26:50.120 --> 00:26:51.120
it, but selling the company.

00:26:51.120 --> 00:26:53.120
I would take a look at that.

00:26:53.120 --> 00:27:01.010
And on the legal cost and then liability side,
I would just say one idea that immediately

00:27:01.010 --> 00:27:06.480
comes to mind, and this is not legal advice,
please speak to your properly vetted attorney

00:27:06.480 --> 00:27:12.950
for this, but potentially there may be the
option of partnering with a larger company

00:27:12.950 --> 00:27:22.100
who can run pilot studies or experiments in
implementing this technology.

00:27:22.100 --> 00:27:24.230
You could have a licensing fee.

00:27:24.230 --> 00:27:25.980
You could have some other type of arrangement.

00:27:25.980 --> 00:27:31.650
They could take a potential equity stake in
the company, although that seems potentially

00:27:31.650 --> 00:27:34.010
premature at this point.

00:27:34.010 --> 00:27:41.530
The reason I bring that up is if they were
willing to test this technology, you could,

00:27:41.530 --> 00:27:47.140
and speak to your lawyer, but you could potentially
be added as an additional insured, so named

00:27:47.140 --> 00:27:51.910
as an additional insured on their insurance
policies.

00:27:51.910 --> 00:27:57.660
It may be relevant to have you added to their
errors and omissions policy, but these are

00:27:57.660 --> 00:28:07.660
ways of being covered by their insurance policies
and therefore the premiums that they pay,

00:28:07.660 --> 00:28:12.860
which in the early stages, if you're bootstrapping,
could be non-trivial.

00:28:12.860 --> 00:28:16.770
Those are just a couple of things that come
to mind in terms of how to launch it.

00:28:16.770 --> 00:28:21.630
One of the benefits, again, of possibly partnering
with larger company is they assume the financial

00:28:21.630 --> 00:28:26.690
risk, a lot of the legal risk of implementing
this and you can see if it actually catches.

00:28:26.690 --> 00:28:27.870
Do people want this?

00:28:27.870 --> 00:28:29.340
Can this company sell it?

00:28:29.340 --> 00:28:33.560
Can this company implement it without assuming
all of those risks yourself?

00:28:33.560 --> 00:28:35.170
All right, let's hop back to the live feed
and see what we got here.

00:28:35.170 --> 00:28:36.170
All right.

00:28:36.170 --> 00:28:37.170
Let me see.

00:28:37.170 --> 00:28:38.170
Seems like we got a couple of things up here.

00:28:38.170 --> 00:28:40.110
Bear with — okay, couple of people asking
about the O2 breathing device that Bas Rutten

00:28:40.110 --> 00:28:41.760
and I had talked about.

00:28:41.760 --> 00:28:46.000
Yes, I have been using it and I've actually
found it tremendously helpful and I don't

00:28:46.000 --> 00:28:47.720
think that's purely placebo effect.

00:28:47.720 --> 00:28:54.150
I used the O2 trainer to prepare for my extensive
hiking at altitude in New Mexico and I found

00:28:54.150 --> 00:29:04.610
it to be very, very helpful and easy to use
and incredibly soreness-inducing.

00:29:04.610 --> 00:29:09.660
When you hear Bas say, with the back breathing
as an example, “Don't start with 30 repetitions,

00:29:09.660 --> 00:29:12.720
start with 15,” take him at his word.

00:29:12.720 --> 00:29:19.870
If you do more than that, you'll feel like
someone hit you with a pickaxe up and down

00:29:19.870 --> 00:29:26.090
all of your muscles around your ribcage and
mid back the next day, or two or three.

00:29:26.090 --> 00:29:33.870
So it is then seemingly very effective for
stressing and applying a workout to your inspiratory

00:29:33.870 --> 00:29:34.870
muscles.

00:29:34.870 --> 00:29:38.460
So I, for one, plan on continuing to use that
device.

00:29:38.460 --> 00:29:39.460
MIDROLL
All right.

00:29:39.460 --> 00:29:40.460
Let's see here.

00:29:40.460 --> 00:29:41.460
Let's see.

00:29:41.460 --> 00:29:45.480
So this is a question from Meade, I hope I'm
getting your name correct, "Would you please

00:29:45.480 --> 00:29:49.710
share any current recommendations that provide
hope or encouragement to a caregiver amid

00:29:49.710 --> 00:29:52.120
a chronic medical situation?"

00:29:52.120 --> 00:29:54.020
Thank you so very much for sharing your experience.

00:29:54.020 --> 00:30:00.230
So first, I'm very sorry that you're in that
situation or someone you know is in that situation.

00:30:00.230 --> 00:30:06.560
And the only advice I can really think of
offhand is, I guess, it's twofold.

00:30:06.560 --> 00:30:13.720
The first is an interview I did with Dr. BJ
Miller quite a few years ago, who is a hospice

00:30:13.720 --> 00:30:20.350
care physician, also a triple amputee who
has helped thousands of people to transition.

00:30:20.350 --> 00:30:27.450
So that conversation, which is on some levels,
deeply philosophical.

00:30:27.450 --> 00:30:34.470
I still act on and revisit a lot from that
conversation.

00:30:34.470 --> 00:30:40.620
I personally found a lot of it incredibly
actionable and impactful.

00:30:40.620 --> 00:30:45.230
So I would suggest potentially giving a listen
to that.

00:30:45.230 --> 00:30:50.780
And secondly, I would recommend a book that
has been recommended to me several times,

00:30:50.780 --> 00:30:57.210
including my friends who have lost parents
unexpectedly, and that is, On Grief and Grieving.

00:30:57.210 --> 00:31:00.390
And I'll find the author right now.

00:31:00.390 --> 00:31:06.040
On Grief and Grieving by David Kessler and
Elisabeth Kübler-Ross.

00:31:06.040 --> 00:31:10.660
And all of the people who have recommended
this book to me have said they wished they

00:31:10.660 --> 00:31:19.170
had read it prior to anyone passing away,
that in fact, it also acts as fantastic preparation

00:31:19.170 --> 00:31:23.650
and consolation if you may be on the verge
of losing someone.

00:31:23.650 --> 00:31:27.710
And we're all on the verge of losing someone
in the grand scheme of things, in the grand

00:31:27.710 --> 00:31:28.710
scheme of life.

00:31:28.710 --> 00:31:31.620
So that would be a second recommendation.

00:31:31.620 --> 00:31:35.990
And apologies if those aren't appropriate,
but given the context I have, those are the

00:31:35.990 --> 00:31:37.460
best I can come up with.

00:31:37.460 --> 00:31:38.460
All right.

00:31:38.460 --> 00:31:43.200
Let me see if I can pull up a few more here.

00:31:43.200 --> 00:31:47.090
So there's a question.

00:31:47.090 --> 00:31:51.320
This is a question from Carrie, "Can you speak
at all about any current information you or

00:31:51.320 --> 00:31:55.960
any other of your previous guests have on
ketamine and bladder damage?"

00:31:55.960 --> 00:32:02.030
So for those who have not heard it, I would
suggest listening to my recent episode with

00:32:02.030 --> 00:32:06.290
Dr. John Krystal, who's chairman of the psychiatry
department at Yale, an incredible researcher

00:32:06.290 --> 00:32:13.570
also in his own right, who along with other
colleagues, pioneered research that identified

00:32:13.570 --> 00:32:20.330
the antidepressant effects of ketamine in
humans, speaking in this case of intravenous

00:32:20.330 --> 00:32:21.810
administration.

00:32:21.810 --> 00:32:31.480
But in that episode, we didn't specifically
discuss bladder damage or urinary tract symptoms.

00:32:31.480 --> 00:32:40.840
And there is quite a bit of literature to
support the assertion that these are potential

00:32:40.840 --> 00:32:45.640
side effects, especially of recreational use
of ketamine and sometimes goes by the name

00:32:45.640 --> 00:32:47.720
ketamine bladder syndrome.

00:32:47.720 --> 00:32:54.440
And I would suggest going to PubMed, if you
just search PubMed, P-U-B-M-E-D, and you search

00:32:54.440 --> 00:33:02.470
ketamine bladder or ketamine urinary tract,
then quite a few reviews, quite a few papers

00:33:02.470 --> 00:33:03.500
will come up.

00:33:03.500 --> 00:33:06.970
So in the abstract here, this is "Ketamine
Bladder Syndrome: An Important Differential

00:33:06.970 --> 00:33:11.700
Diagnosis When Assessing a Patient with Persistent
Lower Urinary Tract Symptoms."

00:33:11.700 --> 00:33:16.640
Abstract, "The recreational use of ketamine
is increasing in popularity due to its dissociative

00:33:16.640 --> 00:33:20.020
and paralytic effects, ease of availability
and low cost."

00:33:20.020 --> 00:33:23.250
This is from 2012, just to timestamp it.

00:33:23.250 --> 00:33:27.430
"However, serious and frequently irreversible
damage to the urinary tract is a recently

00:33:27.430 --> 00:33:30.600
recognized side effect of recreational ketamine
use."

00:33:30.600 --> 00:33:34.240
And this goes on to present a number of case
studies.

00:33:34.240 --> 00:33:40.920
So my understanding is, and this, I'm not
a doctor, I don't play one on the internet,

00:33:40.920 --> 00:33:46.720
but my understanding is that this is reasonably
common in recreational use.

00:33:46.720 --> 00:33:54.470
But I'm not aware how frequently that pops
up as an adverse event in the clinical trials

00:33:54.470 --> 00:33:56.210
that have been done.

00:33:56.210 --> 00:33:59.030
And those would be documented in those studies.

00:33:59.030 --> 00:34:06.470
So you could certainly look for, say John
Krystal, K-R-Y-S-T-A-L on PubMed and look

00:34:06.470 --> 00:34:07.470
at the studies.

00:34:07.470 --> 00:34:12.869
And if you get the complete study, not just
the abstract, you can look at the frequency

00:34:12.869 --> 00:34:19.429
of adverse events and most certainly something
like that would be included, I imagine.

00:34:19.429 --> 00:34:24.990
But you can find more in depth information
on PubMed, which is surprisingly easy to use,

00:34:24.990 --> 00:34:30.349
and I find generally quite user friendly,
even though you may need to look up some words

00:34:30.349 --> 00:34:31.379
now and again.

00:34:31.379 --> 00:34:34.669
Okay, well here's one from Sabrina, "I'm my
own bottleneck in my business.

00:34:34.669 --> 00:34:35.669
Is that bad?"

00:34:35.669 --> 00:34:37.349
It's not automatically bad.

00:34:37.349 --> 00:34:39.010
Depends on what you want to do with your business,
right?

00:34:39.010 --> 00:34:45.289
If you are a artisanal high-end leather pantmaker.

00:34:45.289 --> 00:34:50.589
And I actually read a case study about such
a person who makes super high-end leather

00:34:50.589 --> 00:34:56.409
pants or did at least for people like Sheryl
Crow and Mick Jagger, et cetera, then you

00:34:56.409 --> 00:34:58.240
are an artist.

00:34:58.240 --> 00:34:59.760
You are the business.

00:34:59.760 --> 00:35:04.769
You are the artisan who is producing your
craft.

00:35:04.769 --> 00:35:12.160
And if you choose that and you're accepting
the good with the bad, then I think it's 100

00:35:12.160 --> 00:35:13.160
percent okay.

00:35:13.160 --> 00:35:14.340
Of course, it is.

00:35:14.340 --> 00:35:16.320
It just depends on what you're trying to do.

00:35:16.320 --> 00:35:18.540
And there is no right answer to that.

00:35:18.540 --> 00:35:24.859
I know many business owners who have chosen
not to scale very, very deliberately.

00:35:24.859 --> 00:35:36.069
And I actually find those counter examples
to today's gestalt and drive to scale and

00:35:36.069 --> 00:35:41.039
build, say public companies or companies that
get acquired to be very refreshing because

00:35:41.039 --> 00:35:45.810
it doesn't get as much fanfare.

00:35:45.810 --> 00:35:49.930
But there is a book that I haven't read in
a very long time, but I really enjoyed it

00:35:49.930 --> 00:35:52.210
when I did called Small Giants.

00:35:52.210 --> 00:35:55.099
The subtitle is, Companies That Choose to
Be Great Instead of Big.

00:35:55.099 --> 00:35:57.460
And it is a book by Bo Burlingham, B-U-R-L-I-N-G-H-A-M.

00:35:57.460 --> 00:35:58.460
So Small Giants.

00:35:58.460 --> 00:36:03.221
But I very much enjoyed that book when I read
it.

00:36:03.221 --> 00:36:07.490
And in fact, the example I just gave you could
have come from that book.

00:36:07.490 --> 00:36:10.770
There's a decent chance that it did.

00:36:10.770 --> 00:36:15.079
This question from Al, "What blogs and newsletters
are you excited about reading or receiving

00:36:15.079 --> 00:36:16.089
every day?"

00:36:16.089 --> 00:36:18.230
There's nothing that I receive every day.

00:36:18.230 --> 00:36:25.190
There are at most newsletters that I get once
a week, and there are very few.

00:36:25.190 --> 00:36:31.540
I mean, The Microdose, which is from UC Berkeley,
and gives a synopsis of happenings and new

00:36:31.540 --> 00:36:34.930
events in the psychedelic spaces of interest
to me.

00:36:34.930 --> 00:36:35.960
But very few newsletters.

00:36:35.960 --> 00:36:44.349
And in fact, even with other writers I really,
really enjoy — so for instance, bear with

00:36:44.349 --> 00:36:45.460
me a second.

00:36:45.460 --> 00:36:52.180
So Matt Levine of Bloomberg, for instance,
I think Matt Levine is one of the best writers.

00:36:52.180 --> 00:36:59.050
And by best I mean not just prolific, because
that is not the measure, volume is not the

00:36:59.050 --> 00:37:04.900
measure, word count is not the measure necessarily
of a good writer, but his combination of volume

00:37:04.900 --> 00:37:12.200
and insight, the density of insight to words
per page is staggering.

00:37:12.200 --> 00:37:20.009
Nonetheless, I know that I have three or four
friends who read Matt's work religiously,

00:37:20.009 --> 00:37:28.109
and I depend on them to forward me specific
snippets or specific newsletters that they

00:37:28.109 --> 00:37:32.990
know align with what I am focused on at the
moment.

00:37:32.990 --> 00:37:40.220
But if I were to add say, one other newsletter
to my regular consumption rounds, it would

00:37:40.220 --> 00:37:41.440
probably be from Matt Levine.

00:37:41.440 --> 00:37:44.130
And there are a handful of others I find interesting.

00:37:44.130 --> 00:37:48.049
But there's nothing that I get on a daily
basis, and that is by design.

00:37:48.049 --> 00:37:51.670
A question from Megan, "What are the first
steps you're planning to take on the animal

00:37:51.670 --> 00:37:53.230
communication journey?"

00:37:53.230 --> 00:37:55.170
Well, there are a number of them.

00:37:55.170 --> 00:37:58.910
And for those who don't know, this is something
that came up in my recent conversation with

00:37:58.910 --> 00:38:02.769
Dr. Gabor Maté.

00:38:02.769 --> 00:38:08.840
One of them is identifying, one of the steps
is identifying where animal communication

00:38:08.840 --> 00:38:13.590
can actually be tested, if this makes sense.

00:38:13.590 --> 00:38:16.119
So and this comes up at all sorts of fields.

00:38:16.119 --> 00:38:18.790
If you're trying to find an expert, right?

00:38:18.790 --> 00:38:22.390
How can you identify, let's just take a different
field.

00:38:22.390 --> 00:38:26.980
There are many folks in the US and elsewhere
who claim to be animal tracking experts.

00:38:26.980 --> 00:38:34.369
But how can you as a non-expert in animal
tracking assess if someone is full of shit

00:38:34.369 --> 00:38:35.730
or the real McCoy?

00:38:35.730 --> 00:38:38.970
The short answer is generally you can't.

00:38:38.970 --> 00:38:49.990
But if you look for the exception, and the
exception is any of system or company or organization

00:38:49.990 --> 00:38:56.059
within which trackers are actually tracked
themselves numerically, then you can start

00:38:56.059 --> 00:38:57.950
to get somewhere.

00:38:57.950 --> 00:39:04.950
And in my case, I ended up looking at safari
outfits in Africa that keep records on trackers

00:39:04.950 --> 00:39:11.869
because they could identify, for instance,
that the average for, let's just call it leopard

00:39:11.869 --> 00:39:19.009
tracking, successful leopard tracking during
these hours is 23 percent, but so and so has

00:39:19.009 --> 00:39:23.079
an average of 71 percent as a success rate.

00:39:23.079 --> 00:39:29.940
And you get almost major league baseball statistics,
animal by animal, time of day by time of day

00:39:29.940 --> 00:39:31.340
for these different trackers.

00:39:31.340 --> 00:39:35.890
And then you can start to assess on an objective
level who is actually good.

00:39:35.890 --> 00:39:40.930
This is even more difficult with something
like animal communication depending on how

00:39:40.930 --> 00:39:42.230
we interpret it.

00:39:42.230 --> 00:39:45.140
But I would, I'm starting with, I would say
two things.

00:39:45.140 --> 00:39:50.730
The first is revisiting dog and training and
mammalian training.

00:39:50.730 --> 00:39:57.930
Although a lot of such training is behavioral
shaping and using nonverbal cues.

00:39:57.930 --> 00:40:02.670
I'm okay with that though, by communication,
I'm not limiting that to verbal cues.

00:40:02.670 --> 00:40:05.290
So it could be using a clicker.

00:40:05.290 --> 00:40:11.140
I'm going to be revisiting Susan Garrett,
who is a master dog trainer I had on the podcast.

00:40:11.140 --> 00:40:12.410
How do you know a dog trainer is good?

00:40:12.410 --> 00:40:14.049
Well, for me, I looked for sports.

00:40:14.049 --> 00:40:21.440
So she is a multiple time, if I'm remembering
correctly, national agility champion with

00:40:21.440 --> 00:40:22.559
her dogs.

00:40:22.559 --> 00:40:25.240
And these are objective measures, which I
like.

00:40:25.240 --> 00:40:27.190
I like to start there.

00:40:27.190 --> 00:40:33.710
And then secondly, I'm very interested in
looking at different methods of hunting and

00:40:33.710 --> 00:40:36.749
how hunters interact with animals.

00:40:36.749 --> 00:40:43.039
The most obvious example that comes to mind
for me being elk, where not only are bull

00:40:43.039 --> 00:40:50.390
calls being used as a way of drawing in bull,
but also cow calls of different types.

00:40:50.390 --> 00:40:58.180
And there are actually elk-calling competitions
where people get on stage and are asked by

00:40:58.180 --> 00:41:01.640
a panel of judges to produce different types
of calls.

00:41:01.640 --> 00:41:02.640
And it's mind-boggling.

00:41:02.640 --> 00:41:07.630
I mean, it's very, very, very impressive what
some of these hunters can do.

00:41:07.630 --> 00:41:10.410
So I think those are two places where I'll
start.

00:41:10.410 --> 00:41:14.930
There are other examples that are of interest
to me that I've looked into and will probably

00:41:14.930 --> 00:41:16.770
spend more time on.

00:41:16.770 --> 00:41:24.280
Equine therapy is quite interesting from a,
I suppose more of a physical kinesthetic,

00:41:24.280 --> 00:41:25.619
interactive perspective.

00:41:25.619 --> 00:41:30.220
But those are some of the jumping off points
that I expect I'll spend some more time on.

00:41:30.220 --> 00:41:33.749
A question from Dan, and then I'm going to
come back to the PR and marketing stuff with

00:41:33.749 --> 00:41:35.059
some of the questions I have here.

00:41:35.059 --> 00:41:40.269
Dan, "What is your protocol prior to taking
a three-day fast to get your body ready?"

00:41:40.269 --> 00:41:42.980
So when I fast, again, not medical advice.

00:41:42.980 --> 00:41:45.440
Talk to your doctor before doing any fasting.

00:41:45.440 --> 00:41:48.150
You can't even get approval to do extended
fasting.

00:41:48.150 --> 00:41:53.029
Probably couldn't get IRB approval to do three-day
fast in humans, depending on the circumstance,

00:41:53.029 --> 00:41:54.029
it'd be very hard.

00:41:54.029 --> 00:41:58.540
It's really hard to do scientific research
on extended fasting these days.

00:41:58.540 --> 00:42:07.450
In any case, what I typically do for a three-day
fast is, let's just say my fast is going to

00:42:07.450 --> 00:42:12.000
begin after an early dinner on Thursday night.

00:42:12.000 --> 00:42:15.160
That is typically how I will line things up.

00:42:15.160 --> 00:42:17.490
Don't do your first three-day fast in the
middle of the week.

00:42:17.490 --> 00:42:21.781
It's going to fuck up your whole situation
and you're going to send a lot of emails you're

00:42:21.781 --> 00:42:23.579
going to have to do damage control on.

00:42:23.579 --> 00:42:24.880
So don't do that.

00:42:24.880 --> 00:42:34.260
So leading up to then, if my last meal is,
let's just say Thursday at 6:00 p.m., what

00:42:34.260 --> 00:42:42.519
I will do in the two days prior is I will
get on a sub maintenance calorie ketogenic

00:42:42.519 --> 00:42:43.970
diet.

00:42:43.970 --> 00:42:50.109
So I will be having smaller meals than usual
that are going to be predominantly fat.

00:42:50.109 --> 00:42:55.240
So let's just say hamburger with some cheese
and maybe a side salad, right?

00:42:55.240 --> 00:43:02.109
But I will be consuming fewer calories than
thought necessary to maintain my lean body

00:43:02.109 --> 00:43:03.109
mass.

00:43:03.109 --> 00:43:06.340
And you can calculate resting metabolic rate
and try to figure out what that is based on

00:43:06.340 --> 00:43:08.789
all sorts of factors.

00:43:08.789 --> 00:43:15.900
So lower calorie keto for maybe two days beforehand
with a fair amount of exercise to deplete

00:43:15.900 --> 00:43:17.490
glycogen stores a bit.

00:43:17.490 --> 00:43:24.230
And then last meal would be a smaller keto
meal on Thursday.

00:43:24.230 --> 00:43:27.400
Sleep as long as possible.

00:43:27.400 --> 00:43:33.260
That's part of the reason the timing is the
way I that I set it up.

00:43:33.260 --> 00:43:38.420
And then beginning on Friday, what I try to
do is prior to that, set up Friday for phone

00:43:38.420 --> 00:43:40.289
calls so that I can be moving.

00:43:40.289 --> 00:43:42.549
So I try to get away from my laptop and move.

00:43:42.549 --> 00:43:47.390
This will actually be very, very helpful for
a whole host of reasons.

00:43:47.390 --> 00:43:51.920
But on Friday, as soon as I get up, I get
a huge bottle of water, I fill it full of

00:43:51.920 --> 00:43:57.730
electrolytes like LMNT, which is what I've
been using for a couple years and maybe put

00:43:57.730 --> 00:44:00.680
in a little bit of lemon just for flavoring,
non-caloric.

00:44:00.680 --> 00:44:04.810
And then I will walk and talk.

00:44:04.810 --> 00:44:11.840
So I'll briskly walk for three to five hours
minimum and take my phone calls.

00:44:11.840 --> 00:44:14.549
This will help on a number of levels.

00:44:14.549 --> 00:44:18.960
You will still have some residual glycogen
and you will break down some of that into

00:44:18.960 --> 00:44:21.960
glucose so that your brain can actually function
on Friday.

00:44:21.960 --> 00:44:23.720
And you can have a bit of caffeine.

00:44:23.720 --> 00:44:24.930
Don't overdo the caffeine.

00:44:24.930 --> 00:44:30.190
One of the mistakes that novice fasters make
is they feel a little sluggish so they end

00:44:30.190 --> 00:44:34.530
up having way too much caffeine and then that
inhibits sleep.

00:44:34.530 --> 00:44:37.380
If you do not supplement with electrolytes,
there's a good chance that you're going to

00:44:37.380 --> 00:44:39.299
experience lower back pain.

00:44:39.299 --> 00:44:45.039
I won't get into the mechanisms responsible
for that, but very often you'll have lower

00:44:45.039 --> 00:44:48.630
back pain, especially if you do more than
a three-day fast.

00:44:48.630 --> 00:44:53.430
And you'll have a cholinergic response where
your heart rate is really fast.

00:44:53.430 --> 00:44:57.410
So I always supplement with electrolytes these
days.

00:44:57.410 --> 00:45:00.349
And it's water only.

00:45:00.349 --> 00:45:06.089
Now if you want to have, say, some black coffee
with a little bit of pure heavy cream, a hundred

00:45:06.089 --> 00:45:12.640
percent fat or a little bit of coconut oil,
I don't think that inhibits most of the beneficial

00:45:12.640 --> 00:45:14.039
effects that we're looking for.

00:45:14.039 --> 00:45:15.039
Okay.

00:45:15.039 --> 00:45:18.230
So then that's Friday.

00:45:18.230 --> 00:45:20.589
Then Saturday, again, same deal.

00:45:20.589 --> 00:45:21.589
Right?

00:45:21.589 --> 00:45:23.780
You don't have to walk as much, but it will
still be somewhat helpful.

00:45:23.780 --> 00:45:28.869
And in fact, a little bit of weight training
can feel very good cognitively.

00:45:28.869 --> 00:45:33.069
And then Sunday, you're going to break fast,
right?

00:45:33.069 --> 00:45:35.369
Not at breakfast time, but that night.

00:45:35.369 --> 00:45:42.420
So then Sunday night around, let's just call
it six, seven, whenever, you would have your

00:45:42.420 --> 00:45:44.859
first meal leading out of the fast.

00:45:44.859 --> 00:45:46.730
And there are all sorts of superstition around
this.

00:45:46.730 --> 00:45:49.880
Like, "Oh, you have to eat grated cabbage,
and you have to do this and you've got to

00:45:49.880 --> 00:45:54.270
start with some unpasteurized fruit juice
and this, that, and the other thing."

00:45:54.270 --> 00:46:01.299
My uninformed take or semi-informed take is
that that seems kind of nonsensical to me.

00:46:01.299 --> 00:46:07.319
If we were starving in any circumstance, would
our body and evolutionary drives require us

00:46:07.319 --> 00:46:08.839
to start with shredded cabbage?

00:46:08.839 --> 00:46:11.799
I mean, it just doesn't make any sense to
me.

00:46:11.799 --> 00:46:15.920
So when I break fast, I'm generally breaking
it with another keto meal.

00:46:15.920 --> 00:46:21.440
Because I like to extend some of the effects
of the fast or at least the ketogenic diet

00:46:21.440 --> 00:46:22.569
for a day or two afterwards.

00:46:22.569 --> 00:46:27.569
You don't have to do that, but I will very
often break the fast with something akin to

00:46:27.569 --> 00:46:31.400
the meals that I used for the preparation
for the fast.

00:46:31.400 --> 00:46:37.299
And those are some of the thoughts that I
have on at least how I personally prepare

00:46:37.299 --> 00:46:40.869
for three-day fasts and implement three-day
fasts.

00:46:40.869 --> 00:46:44.400
Your mileage will vary and please get professional
medical advice.

00:46:44.400 --> 00:46:45.730
Let me take a look at some of the questions
that were sent in advance.

00:46:45.730 --> 00:46:50.559
This is a question from Dave, "Are you seeing
any trends in successful nonprofit launches?

00:46:50.559 --> 00:46:54.809
I'm kicking off an effort to generate and
share coastal resilience data."

00:46:54.809 --> 00:46:59.940
All right, so the subject area, coastal resilience
data, certainly of interest to me.

00:46:59.940 --> 00:47:06.829
And what I would say is study trends in the
for-profit world.

00:47:06.829 --> 00:47:12.109
Launching nonprofit ventures, launching for-profit
ventures, principles are exactly the same

00:47:12.109 --> 00:47:13.730
in my experience.

00:47:13.730 --> 00:47:18.240
I think some nonprofits have done a very good
job with promotional efforts.

00:47:18.240 --> 00:47:23.540
I think DonorsChoose and Charity: Water are
two that come to mind immediately that are

00:47:23.540 --> 00:47:27.880
very good and have for a very long time been
very good at this.

00:47:27.880 --> 00:47:29.740
So you might take a look at those.

00:47:29.740 --> 00:47:32.190
But I would really borrow from the private
sector.

00:47:32.190 --> 00:47:39.680
The stakes are generally higher, the talent
and competition more intense in the for-profit

00:47:39.680 --> 00:47:40.680
world.

00:47:40.680 --> 00:47:46.559
So a lot of the mediocre or B minus approaches
will just get massacred before they ever see

00:47:46.559 --> 00:47:47.559
the light of day.

00:47:47.559 --> 00:47:51.019
They won't actually overcome any degree of
competition.

00:47:51.019 --> 00:47:55.410
So I suggest that you look at the for-profit
world.

00:47:55.410 --> 00:48:00.910
Now for fundraising purposes, fundraising
is slightly different than selling a product,

00:48:00.910 --> 00:48:08.249
although I think there's more in common than
there are differences to be perfectly honest.

00:48:08.249 --> 00:48:12.489
But I would look at any number of older examples.

00:48:12.489 --> 00:48:14.619
We don't need to reinvent the world here.

00:48:14.619 --> 00:48:20.190
So what I would say is, and I've mentioned
this book before, but I will repeat it because

00:48:20.190 --> 00:48:22.829
it'll come up again in a second just as a
proof point.

00:48:22.829 --> 00:48:26.200
So I'll give you evidence that this actually
does work.

00:48:26.200 --> 00:48:28.150
The 22 Immutable Laws of Marketing.

00:48:28.150 --> 00:48:32.440
Get the older one, not the for the internet,
which is horribly out of date.

00:48:32.440 --> 00:48:36.190
Get the older one that has the beer examples,
the airline examples.

00:48:36.190 --> 00:48:41.029
The 22 Immutable Laws of Marketing, and take
a close look at that.

00:48:41.029 --> 00:48:46.890
Also read "1,000 True Fans" by Kevin Kelly,
that's on kk.org, "1,000 True Fans."

00:48:46.890 --> 00:48:53.710
And last but not least, read The 80/20 Principle
by Richard Koch, K-O-C-H.

00:48:53.710 --> 00:49:01.849
And the first two of those, "1,000 True Fans"
will take 10 minutes to read, The 22 Immutable

00:49:01.849 --> 00:49:05.499
Laws of Marketing, some of which is not going
to apply, but a lot of which will, will take

00:49:05.499 --> 00:49:07.190
you maybe an hour or two to read.

00:49:07.190 --> 00:49:11.050
And then The 80/20 Principle will probably
take you a day or two to read.

00:49:11.050 --> 00:49:15.660
So you might sequence it in that way, but
I would focus on those.

00:49:15.660 --> 00:49:19.560
And then I'll give you maybe an unorthodox
recommendation.

00:49:19.560 --> 00:49:22.769
But in 5-Bullet Friday, my newsletter.

00:49:22.769 --> 00:49:24.720
If you're not on it, check it out, tim.blog/friday.

00:49:24.720 --> 00:49:28.160
About two million people subscribe.

