Let me take a look at what I included recently
that I found led to a series I was very interested

00:49:36.509 --> 00:49:37.509
in.

00:49:37.509 --> 00:49:38.509
And it is a YouTube series.

00:49:38.509 --> 00:49:39.509
Bear with me a sec.

00:49:39.509 --> 00:49:40.509
Nope, that's not the one.

00:49:40.509 --> 00:49:41.509
Okay.

00:49:41.509 --> 00:49:42.509
Guys, come on.

00:49:42.509 --> 00:49:43.880
Man, I've been doing 5-Bullet Friday for a
long time, every week for I don't know, how

00:49:43.880 --> 00:49:44.880
many years?

00:49:44.880 --> 00:49:46.789
Eight years, nine years, 10 years.

00:49:46.789 --> 00:49:50.690
Okay, so here we go.

00:49:50.690 --> 00:49:58.009
Business Insider has a YouTube video series
about business collapses.

00:49:58.009 --> 00:50:03.270
And one of them I found fascinating called
"How the Beanie Babies Frenzy Collapsed."

00:50:03.270 --> 00:50:05.990
That's an 11-minute video.

00:50:05.990 --> 00:50:10.390
And when you watch "How the Beanie Babies
Frenzy Collapsed," which I recommend, because

00:50:10.390 --> 00:50:15.460
it may relate to a lot of the implosions that
we have seen and will continue to see with

00:50:15.460 --> 00:50:18.880
some of the activities in NFTs and so on.

00:50:18.880 --> 00:50:25.039
But these historical examples can not only
teach you a lot about what doesn't work, but

00:50:25.039 --> 00:50:26.400
about what works.

00:50:26.400 --> 00:50:33.839
So embedded in these how, fill in the blank,
collapsed are certain principles that I think

00:50:33.839 --> 00:50:36.019
you can also borrow and apply.

00:50:36.019 --> 00:50:39.599
So that series by Business Insider I think
is exceptional.

00:50:39.599 --> 00:50:44.140
I looked at a few of the videos and found
them all very, very instructive, and each

00:50:44.140 --> 00:50:47.329
one had a number of takeaways for me personally.

00:50:47.329 --> 00:50:49.299
So I would encourage also taking a look at
that.

00:50:49.299 --> 00:50:50.636
I'll do one more and then I'm going to come
back to the live chat.

00:50:50.636 --> 00:50:51.636
This is going to serve as both a proof point
and a question.

00:50:51.636 --> 00:50:52.890
So this is from Joel, "Ahoy, Tim."

00:50:52.890 --> 00:50:55.579
This is going to be a little long, but I'm
going to read it anyway.

00:50:55.579 --> 00:50:56.579
"I love your Q and As.

00:50:56.579 --> 00:50:59.619
I really appreciate the opportunity to hear
curious questions from the gigantic diversity

00:50:59.619 --> 00:51:00.619
of people you've helped.

00:51:00.619 --> 00:51:01.619
So thank you for this.

00:51:01.619 --> 00:51:05.380
"My question relates to The 22 Immutable Laws
of Marketing by Al Ries and Jack Trout.

00:51:05.380 --> 00:51:07.700
Also, thank you for introducing me to this
book in 2016.

00:51:07.700 --> 00:51:12.619
It has since helped me build a business with
1.8 million lifetime sales from making porcelain

00:51:12.619 --> 00:51:13.619
coffee books."

00:51:13.619 --> 00:51:14.619
Okay.

00:51:14.619 --> 00:51:15.749
So this stuff works, guys.

00:51:15.749 --> 00:51:16.749
It does work.

00:51:16.749 --> 00:51:20.680
And I can't take credit because I learned
from the book as well and have applied it.

00:51:20.680 --> 00:51:25.539
So I'm simply passing along something that
no doubt was introduced to me by any number

00:51:25.539 --> 00:51:26.539
of people.

00:51:26.539 --> 00:51:28.840
All right, back to the description and the
question.

00:51:28.840 --> 00:51:32.170
"The problem is that I handcraft each mug
with my bare hands using porcelain clay and

00:51:32.170 --> 00:51:33.190
a pottery wheel.

00:51:33.190 --> 00:51:36.980
I'm living the dream making a living as an
artist, and I'm obsessed with the art of the

00:51:36.980 --> 00:51:38.570
pottery mug, but it's pretty hard.

00:51:38.570 --> 00:51:40.989
And I'm still working on long term sustainable
profits.

00:51:40.989 --> 00:51:44.180
I've thought about outsourcing to China, the
porcelain capital of the world, but that's

00:51:44.180 --> 00:51:45.180
fraught with problems.

00:51:45.180 --> 00:51:49.059
"So instead of trying to grow by selling billions
of cheap mugs, I've decided to grow my business

00:51:49.059 --> 00:51:51.520
by handcrafting mugs worthy of a higher price."

00:51:51.520 --> 00:51:53.480
I agree with this approach.

00:51:53.480 --> 00:51:56.470
I think that you do not want to be engaged
in a race to the bottom.

00:51:56.470 --> 00:52:00.510
"However," back to the text, "That means creating
a sort of luxury brand.

00:52:00.510 --> 00:52:05.239
Since this doesn't exist today, we're focusing
on law number one, the law of leadership that

00:52:05.239 --> 00:52:07.609
says it's better to be first than it is to
be better.

00:52:07.609 --> 00:52:10.989
These mugs are art, but they're not better
than the world's best sculptures and paintings,

00:52:10.989 --> 00:52:16.099
which often sell for millions of dollars each
or fancy-ass, diamond-encrusted purses that

00:52:16.099 --> 00:52:18.280
sell for tens of thousands of dollars each.

00:52:18.280 --> 00:52:21.579
But I believe it could be the first mug to
inspire people globally as a piece of art

00:52:21.579 --> 00:52:23.590
that you can enjoy your morning coffee from
every day.

00:52:23.590 --> 00:52:27.480
Law number four, the law perception has been
one factor that helped me sell mugs for 500

00:52:27.480 --> 00:52:29.160
to 1,000 dollars per mug."

00:52:29.160 --> 00:52:30.300
Nicely done.

00:52:30.300 --> 00:52:33.549
"But the vast majority of customers want a
cheaper mug.

00:52:33.549 --> 00:52:36.710
How would you evaluate whether or not a luxury
mug business is worth pursuing?

00:52:36.710 --> 00:52:40.530
And if you determined it was worth pursuing,
what would you do to grow?"

00:52:40.530 --> 00:52:41.750
All right.

00:52:41.750 --> 00:52:44.190
Let's hop into this.

00:52:44.190 --> 00:52:48.660
So there are a few books I would recommend
checking out, Joel.

00:52:48.660 --> 00:52:50.910
As usual, I have many book recommendations.

00:52:50.910 --> 00:52:55.489
So the first, which I've not read in a very,
very long time, but may be helpful, is a book

00:52:55.489 --> 00:52:58.859
called Selling to the Affluent.

00:52:58.859 --> 00:53:01.460
That's by Thomas Stanley.

00:53:01.460 --> 00:53:05.869
And you may be able to find a summary and
to determine if it is in any way relevant

00:53:05.869 --> 00:53:06.869
to you.

00:53:06.869 --> 00:53:11.230
One way that I assess books oftentimes is
trying to find a Wikipedia page for the book,

00:53:11.230 --> 00:53:14.070
which will give you a synopsis of some type.

00:53:14.070 --> 00:53:18.280
So Selling to the Affluent by Thomas J. Stanley,
I found certainly helpful at one point when

00:53:18.280 --> 00:53:22.779
I was considering many of the same questions
for different businesses.

00:53:22.779 --> 00:53:31.990
The other book may sound a little funny at
first because it would seem to be anti advice,

00:53:31.990 --> 00:53:40.200
but it is called Deluxe, subtitle, How Luxury
Lost Its Luster.

00:53:40.200 --> 00:53:41.970
There's a lot of alliteration here.

00:53:41.970 --> 00:53:45.480
It's hard for my tongue to get out right now.

00:53:45.480 --> 00:53:47.329
But this book is by Dana Thomas.

00:53:47.329 --> 00:53:48.839
I hope I'm getting the pronunciation right.

00:53:48.839 --> 00:53:53.130
Deluxe is the name of the book, How Luxury
Lost Its Luster.

00:53:53.130 --> 00:54:00.099
And it talks about different high-end luxury
brands and how some of them have compromised

00:54:00.099 --> 00:54:06.770
themselves by outsourcing supply chains and
making other poor strategic decisions.

00:54:06.770 --> 00:54:15.420
But embedded through the whole story and the
various case studies is the cocktail of ingredients

00:54:15.420 --> 00:54:21.401
that have made some of the most durable luxury
brands the world has ever seen, at least in

00:54:21.401 --> 00:54:23.030
the last several centuries.

00:54:23.030 --> 00:54:25.339
So I really greatly enjoyed this book.

00:54:25.339 --> 00:54:28.289
It's got 3.9 out of five on Goodreads.

00:54:28.289 --> 00:54:34.630
For your particular case, it's about 400 pages
long, I think that is worth reading for a

00:54:34.630 --> 00:54:38.730
whole host of reasons.

00:54:38.730 --> 00:54:45.509
And then what I would say is to your last
two questions, "How would you evaluate whether

00:54:45.509 --> 00:54:48.589
or not a luxury mug businesses were pursuing?"

00:54:48.589 --> 00:54:54.210
So I think that, and I have this predisposition
to, it's very tempting to try to figure it

00:54:54.210 --> 00:54:55.210
out, right?

00:54:55.210 --> 00:55:00.119
To sit down with pen and paper, look at a
few databases and decide, is this worth doing?

00:55:00.119 --> 00:55:01.819
Is it not worth doing?

00:55:01.819 --> 00:55:04.320
That can be very time consuming.

00:55:04.320 --> 00:55:05.670
It can also be misleading.

00:55:05.670 --> 00:55:10.430
Maybe you have, or actually certainly you'll
have partial information.

00:55:10.430 --> 00:55:15.839
The question I would reflect back to you is
how could you test in the real world whether

00:55:15.839 --> 00:55:18.890
or not this is worth pursuing, right?

00:55:18.890 --> 00:55:23.880
So if you had pick a budget that you're willing
to lose, five grand, 10 grand, how could you

00:55:23.880 --> 00:55:31.260
use that to determine, to test the waters,
to see if people are willing to buy whatever

00:55:31.260 --> 00:55:32.410
you have to sell?

00:55:32.410 --> 00:55:34.839
And so what does that mean, luxury mug business?

00:55:34.839 --> 00:55:35.839
Right?

00:55:35.839 --> 00:55:39.470
If you're selling mugs for 500 to a thousand
per mug, I don't know what changes in order

00:55:39.470 --> 00:55:41.200
to make it a luxury mug business.

00:55:41.200 --> 00:55:42.990
Does that mean $5,000 per mug?

00:55:42.990 --> 00:55:44.079
Okay, fine.

00:55:44.079 --> 00:55:46.079
10,000, whatever the number is.

00:55:46.079 --> 00:55:50.609
There are people out there who will buy such
mugs.

00:55:50.609 --> 00:55:56.920
Another question I would just add to this
is what story do these luxury mug buyers get

00:55:56.920 --> 00:55:59.130
to tell their friends?

00:55:59.130 --> 00:56:03.660
If you look at the contemporary art world,
you will see, I'm not saying this is the same,

00:56:03.660 --> 00:56:05.359
but I think there are overlaps.

00:56:05.359 --> 00:56:08.059
The story fucking matters a lot, right?

00:56:08.059 --> 00:56:12.710
You can't just tape a banana to a wall at
Art Basel with duct tape and have no story.

00:56:12.710 --> 00:56:14.809
There needs to be a story.

00:56:14.809 --> 00:56:24.270
And there are many documentaries that you
could consider watching.

00:56:24.270 --> 00:56:36.249
The Price of Everything is a documentary about
contemporary art, and I found it very, very

00:56:36.249 --> 00:56:37.249
engaging.

00:56:37.249 --> 00:56:40.800
And there are many contrasting styles in here.

00:56:40.800 --> 00:56:42.339
So check it out.

00:56:42.339 --> 00:56:45.539
Here's the description, "With unprecedented
access to pivotal artists and the white hot

00:56:45.539 --> 00:56:49.059
market surrounding them, The Price of Everything
dives deep into the labyrinth of the contemporary

00:56:49.059 --> 00:56:50.059
art world.

00:56:50.059 --> 00:56:53.809
It examines the role of art and artistic passion
in today's money-driven, consumer-based society

00:56:53.809 --> 00:56:56.769
where everything can be bought and sold."

00:56:56.769 --> 00:56:58.070
I really enjoyed this.

00:56:58.070 --> 00:56:59.569
I really, really enjoyed it.

00:56:59.569 --> 00:57:07.150
So this would be another recommendation because
there's again, no need to reinvent the wheel.

00:57:07.150 --> 00:57:12.480
I mean, these are not necessarily identically
the people you are going to be selling to,

00:57:12.480 --> 00:57:16.710
but what does it take to sell the highest-priced
X?

00:57:16.710 --> 00:57:19.479
Fill in the blank, whatever that is.

00:57:19.479 --> 00:57:29.329
And in fact, some of my answers are going
to be overlapping with a question from a listener

00:57:29.329 --> 00:57:30.460
in Brazil.

00:57:30.460 --> 00:57:36.450
Carlos, "I'd like to know your thoughts about
how to market a product design to high profile

00:57:36.450 --> 00:57:39.320
people, consultancy for professional soccer
players.

00:57:39.320 --> 00:57:40.369
Which channel would you recommend?

00:57:40.369 --> 00:57:43.160
Or are there some case studies to which you'd
refer?

00:57:43.160 --> 00:57:44.170
Thank you for everything.

00:57:44.170 --> 00:57:45.410
Big fan from Brazil."

00:57:45.410 --> 00:57:47.160
Okay.

00:57:47.160 --> 00:57:52.270
So all of the books, Carlos, this all apply
to you too, all of the books and the documentaries

00:57:52.270 --> 00:57:53.849
I recommended would apply.

00:57:53.849 --> 00:57:58.809
Another thing that you can do is study luxury
brands.

00:57:58.809 --> 00:58:00.910
Figure out who their PR firms are.

00:58:00.910 --> 00:58:06.630
Go to the PR firm websites and look at their
campaign case studies.

00:58:06.630 --> 00:58:08.200
See what they do.

00:58:08.200 --> 00:58:09.480
Right?

00:58:09.480 --> 00:58:11.700
And you can learn a lot by doing this.

00:58:11.700 --> 00:58:17.150
And you can create, in the old days, what
was called a swipe file, where you find a,

00:58:17.150 --> 00:58:23.019
for many years, whenever an ad convinced me
to spend money I shouldn't spend.

00:58:23.019 --> 00:58:31.180
So let's just say back when I was making 35,
40 grand a year, if an ad came across that

00:58:31.180 --> 00:58:35.930
convinced me to buy something that was $500,
that was a lot of money to me.

00:58:35.930 --> 00:58:41.740
And if it actually convinced me to even pick
up the phone or get online to buy it, whether

00:58:41.740 --> 00:58:44.779
or not I completed the purchase, I would keep
that.

00:58:44.779 --> 00:58:49.630
I would tear it out of the magazine or I would
take a photograph of it, or I would otherwise

00:58:49.630 --> 00:58:51.619
try to record it in some fashion.

00:58:51.619 --> 00:58:52.859
Let's say it was a radio advertisement.

00:58:52.859 --> 00:58:57.299
I'd try to remember the text and type it out
and I would create a three ring binder of

00:58:57.299 --> 00:59:04.470
all the things that almost or did convince
me to spend in an inordinate, let's just call

00:59:04.470 --> 00:59:09.739
it unreasonable percentage of my after tax
income.

00:59:09.739 --> 00:59:13.019
And that is another thing that you can start
to do now.

00:59:13.019 --> 00:59:17.299
You do not need to be a professional market
analyst to do this, you just need to be aware

00:59:17.299 --> 00:59:22.410
of your own behavior and what drives you to
purchase decisions or near purchase decisions.

00:59:22.410 --> 00:59:28.210
So just to reiterate something I just said,
brands you admire that do a good job, campaigns

00:59:28.210 --> 00:59:33.750
that are doing a good job or individuals who
are doing a good job with selling into luxury

00:59:33.750 --> 00:59:34.750
markets.

00:59:34.750 --> 00:59:40.460
Study what they do, figure out who their publicists
are, figure out who their PR firms are, and

00:59:40.460 --> 00:59:43.390
then study those publicists and PR firms.

00:59:43.390 --> 00:59:48.489
Most of the time, both of those categories
are going to be providing campaign examples

00:59:48.489 --> 00:59:49.799
and so on.

00:59:49.799 --> 00:59:53.910
You may even be able to get on the phone to
have an exploratory conversation with these

00:59:53.910 --> 00:59:54.910
people.

00:59:54.910 --> 00:59:58.420
All right, so those are a number of examples.

00:59:58.420 --> 01:00:05.819
The other question that is worth reflecting
back, and this applies to Joel as well as

01:00:05.819 --> 01:00:12.239
Carlos, that I reverse engineer a lot for
myself, is set the price first and then make

01:00:12.239 --> 01:00:13.339
it worth it.

01:00:13.339 --> 01:00:17.309
So for instance, when I had an event, and
I never do events.

01:00:17.309 --> 01:00:18.319
I never produce events.

01:00:18.319 --> 01:00:22.470
They're such a pain in the ass, but I did
produce one very high end event and I decided

01:00:22.470 --> 01:00:27.980
that I wanted to charge, I think it was minimum
$10,000 a seat, and then it went up from there.

01:00:27.980 --> 01:00:32.690
So the sooner somebody bought the less expensive
it was, and then it just went up in price.

01:00:32.690 --> 01:00:34.349
So the minimum was $10,000.

01:00:34.349 --> 01:00:39.239
I started with that as a nice round number,
and it was, I think a two or three-day event.

01:00:39.239 --> 01:00:42.380
Then I thought to myself, All right, I want
to charge $10,000.

01:00:42.380 --> 01:00:47.080
How can I deliver that much value in the first
two hours that people are there, so that I

01:00:47.080 --> 01:00:48.080
don't have to worry about it?

01:00:48.080 --> 01:00:53.780
How can I overdeliver in such a way in the
first hour or two that everything else is

01:00:53.780 --> 01:00:54.780
gravy?

01:00:54.780 --> 01:00:58.460
Everyone is thrilled they came within the
first two hours.

01:00:58.460 --> 01:01:01.539
That's a creative exercise, and I was able
to pull it off.

01:01:01.539 --> 01:01:07.729
That is because I decided on an ultra high
luxury price and then asked myself, How on

01:01:07.729 --> 01:01:10.099
earth can I make it worth it?

01:01:10.099 --> 01:01:14.720
What are the different ideas I have for making
it worth it?

01:01:14.720 --> 01:01:21.299
Ultimately, it came down to sharing the actual
book proposals that I used for The 4-Hour

01:01:21.299 --> 01:01:26.519
Workweek and The 4-Hour Body in entirety with
a few things redacted just for sensitivity,

01:01:26.519 --> 01:01:32.990
and then also acting as a matchmaker for everyone
in the room for the period of about an hour

01:01:32.990 --> 01:01:35.029
to 90 minutes.

01:01:35.029 --> 01:01:40.380
After that, I mean, everybody was set in some
respect.

01:01:40.380 --> 01:01:45.880
For those people who might want to borrow
this format, each person got up and the format

01:01:45.880 --> 01:01:48.109
was give a brag.

01:01:48.109 --> 01:01:52.259
You have to give a brag about yourself, so
you have to brag in some way.

01:01:52.259 --> 01:01:55.160
Then you have an ask, and then you have a
give.

01:01:55.160 --> 01:01:58.900
So everybody in the audience, I think there
were a hundred or 120 people got up and said,

01:01:58.900 --> 01:02:00.369
This is who I am.

01:02:00.369 --> 01:02:03.711
Here's one thing I want to brag about, or
maybe I don't want to brag about, but since

01:02:03.711 --> 01:02:06.440
it's a directive, I will brag about.

01:02:06.440 --> 01:02:08.749
Here's one thing that is an ask.

01:02:08.749 --> 01:02:10.640
This is what I'm looking for.

01:02:10.640 --> 01:02:14.269
And then here's one thing that I can give
that I'm really good at, or relationships

01:02:14.269 --> 01:02:18.150
that I have, expertise, whatever it might
be.

01:02:18.150 --> 01:02:19.599
I just encouraged everybody to take notes.

01:02:19.599 --> 01:02:23.049
If you can help somebody who has an ask, go
help them.

01:02:23.049 --> 01:02:29.349
If you need something that they have on offer,
because that's a give, go find them.

01:02:29.349 --> 01:02:31.019
The rest of the event took care of itself.

01:02:31.019 --> 01:02:34.329
But I would not have come up with those answers
had I not asked the right question.

01:02:34.329 --> 01:02:35.489
It comes down to the right question.

01:02:35.489 --> 01:02:39.160
So start with the price that makes it work.

01:02:39.160 --> 01:02:40.559
Joel, luxury mug business.

01:02:40.559 --> 01:02:42.150
What does that mean?

01:02:42.150 --> 01:02:44.970
How many mugs at what price?

01:02:44.970 --> 01:02:47.609
Let's just say your goal is a hundred thousand
dollars a year.

01:02:47.609 --> 01:02:51.420
Well, the answer could take many, many, many,
many different forms.

01:02:51.420 --> 01:02:52.470
Is it a thousand?

01:02:52.470 --> 01:02:55.249
Well, let me step back.

01:02:55.249 --> 01:02:56.539
We'll make it a million dollars.

01:02:56.539 --> 01:03:00.289
Let's get ambitious, million dollars a year.

01:03:00.289 --> 01:03:04.859
That could be a thousand mugs at a thousand
dollars a piece.

01:03:04.859 --> 01:03:13.549
It could be one mug at a million dollars,
or it could be somewhere in the middle.

01:03:13.549 --> 01:03:16.059
How can you mitigate risk in these experiments?

01:03:16.059 --> 01:03:22.470
Well, look, if you look at different examples
of collaborations, you have, for instance,

01:03:22.470 --> 01:03:33.170
CryptoPunks, now owned by Yuga Labs, partnering
with Tiffany to create bedazzled, customized

01:03:33.170 --> 01:03:36.279
CryptoPunk jewelry.

01:03:36.279 --> 01:03:37.599
How the fuck do these things happen?

01:03:37.599 --> 01:03:40.049
It's worth digging into, trying to find out
how those things happen.

01:03:40.049 --> 01:03:41.049
What are the mechanics?

01:03:41.049 --> 01:03:42.049
What are the agreements?

01:03:42.049 --> 01:03:45.410
What are the responsibilities of the various
parties?

01:03:45.410 --> 01:03:47.859
Not to belabor that one example.

01:03:47.859 --> 01:03:50.460
There are many such examples.

01:03:50.460 --> 01:03:59.170
So for instance, how could you potentially
have one or 10 or a hundred of these go into

01:03:59.170 --> 01:04:03.229
gift bags at the Oscars or at the Golden Globes,
or fill in the blank?

01:04:03.229 --> 01:04:05.050
This doesn't automatically work, by the way.

01:04:05.050 --> 01:04:07.920
I have had my books put into such bags.

01:04:07.920 --> 01:04:10.569
Sometimes it has amazing effects, sometimes
it doesn't.

01:04:10.569 --> 01:04:14.529
Sometimes it has amazing effects you don't
even know of until 10 years later.

01:04:14.529 --> 01:04:19.020
So be prepared to play the long game if you're
going to do anything like that.

01:04:19.020 --> 01:04:21.619
You have to be prepared for your investment
to go to zero.

01:04:21.619 --> 01:04:27.000
So if your material cost and your labor cost
is 10 grand, maybe it's worth rolling the

01:04:27.000 --> 01:04:28.000
dice.

01:04:28.000 --> 01:04:31.599
If it's a hundred grand, maybe not, depending
on your financial picture.

01:04:31.599 --> 01:04:33.789
