01:07:10.040 --> 01:07:11.329
All right, let me take a look what we got
here.

01:07:11.329 --> 01:07:15.469
All right, Josh, I'll keep this short, but
if you wanted to displace/outcompete an established

01:07:15.469 --> 01:07:19.700
tech company with a similar product, how would
you approach it from a marketing lens, assuming

01:07:19.700 --> 01:07:21.359
you had no influence on product?

01:07:21.359 --> 01:07:23.280
This is going to sound funny.

01:07:23.280 --> 01:07:27.720
It's straight from The 22 Immutable Laws of
Marketing, but I would study Avis.

01:07:27.720 --> 01:07:31.969
"We try harder" versus Hertz way back in the
day.

01:07:31.969 --> 01:07:37.109
The strategy was more sophisticated than just
that tagline.

01:07:37.109 --> 01:07:42.329
If you're truly similar and undifferentiated,
I think you're going to have a hard time,

01:07:42.329 --> 01:07:47.460
and you're probably going to have to compete
on price, which is a dangerous game because

01:07:47.460 --> 01:07:51.250
incumbents with much more money can say, You
know what?

01:07:51.250 --> 01:07:53.460
We have 12 product lines.

01:07:53.460 --> 01:07:54.869
We don't like this competitor.

01:07:54.869 --> 01:08:01.160
We don't want competition, so we are just
going to sell below our cost for a period

01:08:01.160 --> 01:08:04.819
of time to bleed them of chips until they
go out of business.

01:08:04.819 --> 01:08:12.539
This is a very, very textbook common competitive
approach that incumbents use to spectacular

01:08:12.539 --> 01:08:14.869
success against startups.

01:08:14.869 --> 01:08:21.069
So it is a risky gambit to compete on price,
but that may be necessary at least for a period

01:08:21.069 --> 01:08:23.109
of time.

01:08:23.109 --> 01:08:31.290
But beyond that, in terms of out competing
or out marketing, I would say you could do

01:08:31.290 --> 01:08:32.850
that on a whole lot of different levels.

01:08:32.850 --> 01:08:38.310
I would study the Avis versus Hertz example,
and you could also study, for instance — now

01:08:38.310 --> 01:08:43.410
this is going to be a price dominant example,
but I read the biography.

01:08:43.410 --> 01:08:49.360
It may have been the autobiography of the
founder of Ryanair at one point, and looked

01:08:49.360 --> 01:08:55.500
at, even though I have no interest in participating
in the airline businesses, looking at how

01:08:55.500 --> 01:09:01.970
low cost competitors and carriers entered
the scene and became at least at points as

01:09:01.970 --> 01:09:03.500
dominant as they did.

01:09:03.500 --> 01:09:06.530
easyJet is another very, very interesting
example.

01:09:06.530 --> 01:09:13.000
Certainly Southwest, another fascinating example,
and I would encourage you to look at all of

01:09:13.000 --> 01:09:16.320
those, even though they may not seem immediately
applicable.

01:09:16.320 --> 01:09:20.320
So those are a few approaches that you might
take.

01:09:20.320 --> 01:09:27.150
But the commonality here is looking for comparables,
not just within your industry, but outside

01:09:27.150 --> 01:09:28.510
of your industry.

01:09:28.510 --> 01:09:36.090
So which startups can you model that have
taken on incumbents with similar products?

01:09:36.090 --> 01:09:40.890
Hopefully not entirely identical, but somewhat
similar products.

01:09:40.890 --> 01:09:49.690
You can also use — I'm not saying you have
to do this, but sort of humor and sass is

01:09:49.690 --> 01:09:54.060
generally going to get neutralized and killed
in any bureaucracy within a bigger company,

01:09:54.060 --> 01:10:03.560
which is why, say, Dollar Shave Club and others
had a tremendous window of opportunity that

01:10:03.560 --> 01:10:09.630
they took excellent advantage of with their
advertising campaigns.

01:10:09.630 --> 01:10:16.920
I think even though one could consider certainly
Richard Branson, an incumbent in some respects,

01:10:16.920 --> 01:10:22.410
and he's certainly not having to bootstrap
things with limited savings.

01:10:22.410 --> 01:10:28.560
If you look at how he competed, and this is
chronicled quite a bit in Losing My Virginity,

01:10:28.560 --> 01:10:32.980
which was one of the books that had a huge
impact on me when I was in my early twenties.

01:10:32.980 --> 01:10:37.340
So Losing My Virginity by Richard Branson
talks about competing in the music business,

01:10:37.340 --> 01:10:42.040
and it also talks about competing against
British Airways with Virgin.

01:10:42.040 --> 01:10:48.461
So it's just happenstance, I suppose, that
a lot of these examples are airlines, but

01:10:48.461 --> 01:10:54.480
in a sense, airlines are the perfect example
of largely undifferentiated competition.

01:10:54.480 --> 01:10:58.600
So it might provide some variables and levers
that you can pull and play with.

