01:15:56.960 --> 01:15:58.850
Specifically looking at traditional longbow?"

01:15:58.850 --> 01:16:04.600
Yes, I have looked quite closely at archery,
and I've been spending the last several months

01:16:04.600 --> 01:16:08.210
going deep down quite a few rabbit holes related
to archery.

01:16:08.210 --> 01:16:15.270
I have one, two, three, four, five — five
or six bows.

01:16:15.270 --> 01:16:21.300
No, more, seven, eight, probably eight bows
within 500 feet of where I'm sitting.

01:16:21.300 --> 01:16:22.770
So yes, I've been getting into it.

01:16:22.770 --> 01:16:29.040
That includes a traditional longbow made by
someone named Byron Ferguson, who has a lot

01:16:29.040 --> 01:16:34.580
of incredible video online.

01:16:34.580 --> 01:16:40.410
I believe Byron has a book called Become the
Arrow.

01:16:40.410 --> 01:16:47.030
Let me see, Yeah, Become the Arrow, second
edition by Byron Ferguson.

01:16:47.030 --> 01:16:53.300
He has some of the most incredible longbow
shots I've ever seen.

01:16:53.300 --> 01:17:01.070
He is the one, as far as I know, who was first
to develop the trick shot progression of shooting

01:17:01.070 --> 01:17:07.410
a disc out of the air that is about the size
of, say, a bread plate, and then shooting

01:17:07.410 --> 01:17:14.920
a golf ball out of the air, and then shooting
a lifesaver out of the air, and then shooting

01:17:14.920 --> 01:17:19.220
an aspirin out of the air using field points.

01:17:19.220 --> 01:17:21.020
So he's quite incredible.

01:17:21.020 --> 01:17:26.580
Longbow, I think is very, very interesting,
very, very challenging also.

01:17:26.580 --> 01:17:35.140
I am quite interested in both Olympic recurve
and bear bow competition.

01:17:35.140 --> 01:17:38.120
So I'm looking at both of those.

01:17:38.120 --> 01:17:45.060
Most of my experience from a precision standpoint
is with compound bows.

01:17:45.060 --> 01:17:51.810
But from the looking at a spectrum of say,
aiming.

01:17:51.810 --> 01:17:57.640
Let's just call it aiming on one end of the
slider and then biomechanics on the far other

01:17:57.640 --> 01:18:04.680
end of the slider, I would say that compound
bow, for me at least, has the most in common

01:18:04.680 --> 01:18:10.200
with rifle or firearm marksmanship.

01:18:10.200 --> 01:18:15.290
So if I were going to train someone for compound,
and I'm not a professional by any means, but

01:18:15.290 --> 01:18:20.610
just to help someone get accustomed to breath
control and aligning front rear sites, I would

01:18:20.610 --> 01:18:27.090
actually have them start with prone rifle
shooting and move from there to compound.

01:18:27.090 --> 01:18:29.720
There are a million different types of releases.

01:18:29.720 --> 01:18:35.750
We won't get into that, but if you have compound
there, and then on the far other end of the

01:18:35.750 --> 01:18:44.460
spectrum with biomechanics, you have longbow,
then I would put something like Olympic recurve

01:18:44.460 --> 01:18:46.570
close to the middle, I would say.

01:18:46.570 --> 01:18:53.010
And then bear bow would be somewhere between
the Olympic recurve and the longbow.

01:18:53.010 --> 01:18:58.810
But the further you get to the biomechanics
side, the better your shot sequence.

01:18:58.810 --> 01:19:03.160
People are going to disagree with me on this,
but the more you get to the biomechanics side,

01:19:03.160 --> 01:19:08.670
the better your body awareness and kinesthetic
control and sequencing of your shot sequence

01:19:08.670 --> 01:19:10.280
will need to be.

01:19:10.280 --> 01:19:16.960
I think that's probably most easily illustrated
by watching video of say, Korean archers in

01:19:16.960 --> 01:19:18.510
Olympic recurve.

01:19:18.510 --> 01:19:21.080
They're just unbelievably good.

01:19:21.080 --> 01:19:22.530
So take a look at that.

01:19:22.530 --> 01:19:26.600
But yes, I'm very interested in archery is
the short answer.

