01:21:24.580 --> 01:21:30.870
The reason I bring those three up is that
when I feel I have been hit unexpectedly with

01:21:30.870 --> 01:21:37.230
something, or perhaps I have precipitated
something happening that is undesirable, my

01:21:37.230 --> 01:21:43.140
defaults, and I think this is from a lot of
family history as well, are anger.

01:21:43.140 --> 01:21:47.930
Mostly anger directed at myself, but anger
and self-flagellation.

01:21:47.930 --> 01:21:56.540
Just incredible and beating myself up mercilessly.

01:21:56.540 --> 01:22:05.000
For those reasons, I mention those guests
because for me, it's not the events that are

01:22:05.000 --> 01:22:06.650
the problem per se.

01:22:06.650 --> 01:22:17.220
It's my reflexive response to those events
being self-abuse through inner monologue or

01:22:17.220 --> 01:22:24.140
dialogue and being able to, number one, create
a greater awareness of that.

01:22:24.140 --> 01:22:31.510
Number two, developing, or borrowing in these
cases, toolkits and questions that allow me

01:22:31.510 --> 01:22:38.020
to calm that reactivity and not to push it
away necessarily, but certainly in the case

01:22:38.020 --> 01:22:43.060
of Tara Brach, to befriend in a way, to have
a conversation with it or with those parts

01:22:43.060 --> 01:22:44.480
is very helpful.

01:22:44.480 --> 01:22:51.590
Another person I would add to that is Richard
Schwartz, who is the founder of IFS.

01:22:51.590 --> 01:22:52.940
I had him on the podcast.

01:22:52.940 --> 01:22:55.190
So those are the first few names that come
to mind.

