01:27:40.850 --> 01:27:41.850
Okay, this is a question from Al.

01:27:41.850 --> 01:27:47.920
"If you had to build a launch strategy for
The 4-Hour Workweek in 2022 versus 20007,

01:27:47.920 --> 01:27:51.320
otherwise known, 2007, what strategies and
channels would you employ?

01:27:51.320 --> 01:27:55.220
Which strategies would stay consistent from
2007 and why?"

01:27:55.220 --> 01:28:03.710
I think the crux decision, which was to engage
with people in person at events, at the time,

01:28:03.710 --> 01:28:04.940
those were predominantly bloggers.

01:28:04.940 --> 01:28:09.740
Now that might be different, but not necessarily.

01:28:09.740 --> 01:28:13.400
Blogs are still actually a thing, despite
what people may think.

01:28:13.400 --> 01:28:22.050
But that core approach to taking the uncrowded
channel of engaging with people in person

01:28:22.050 --> 01:28:27.510
at events, in recreational settings over drinks
would remain consistent.

01:28:27.510 --> 01:28:34.780
You could remove every other prong of The
4-Hour Workweek launch strategy.

01:28:34.780 --> 01:28:41.490
If I simply executed well again on that type
of in person engagement, I think the book

01:28:41.490 --> 01:28:46.080
would still be a success today.

01:28:46.080 --> 01:28:52.380
To reiterate how I did that, or to give one
example, I would go to the after party after

01:28:52.380 --> 01:28:56.050
some conference event where people are on
panels, and sometimes I would approach them

01:28:56.050 --> 01:28:58.060
afterwards.

01:28:58.060 --> 01:29:01.580
Very often when I would approach a panel,
I would not approach the panelists who are

01:29:01.580 --> 01:29:02.580
getting mobbed.

01:29:02.580 --> 01:29:09.300
I would approach the moderator who is deservedly
neglected a lot of times in cases like this,

01:29:09.300 --> 01:29:14.690
and chat with the moderator, usually asking
a handful of follow up questions, which are

01:29:14.690 --> 01:29:17.460
sincere questions about the topics on the
panel.

01:29:17.460 --> 01:29:20.940
They would at some point generally ask me,
"Well, what are you doing here?

01:29:20.940 --> 01:29:21.940
Why are you here?"

01:29:21.940 --> 01:29:23.690
And I would say, "It's my first time at this
event.

01:29:23.690 --> 01:29:26.610
I've just finished writing my first book.

01:29:26.610 --> 01:29:27.820
It's this, this, and this.

01:29:27.820 --> 01:29:34.740
It's based on guest lectures I gave at Princeton
University from 2000 x to 2000 y.

01:29:34.740 --> 01:29:38.730
I'm trying to figure out how to launch a book
since it's really on me.

01:29:38.730 --> 01:29:41.200
The publisher effectively prints and ships
books.

01:29:41.200 --> 01:29:43.190
I have to figure out how to market this.

01:29:43.190 --> 01:29:45.750
Is there anyone you think I might want to
meet?

01:29:45.750 --> 01:29:48.590
Is there anyone you think I might get along
with?"

01:29:48.590 --> 01:29:52.490
Then they would make a referral and I would
be on to the next referral.

01:29:52.490 --> 01:29:55.900
I would repeat something like that each time.

01:29:55.900 --> 01:30:00.750
Separately, if I came upon, say, a group of
people, bloggers, let's just say.

01:30:00.750 --> 01:30:03.910
I knew at least one blogger was in this group,
at a bar.

01:30:03.910 --> 01:30:08.400
They'd be talking, and I would wander up and
I would say something like, "Hey, guys, do

01:30:08.400 --> 01:30:12.070
you mind if I join in and just listen in?"

01:30:12.070 --> 01:30:13.910
And they'd be like, "Whatever.

01:30:13.910 --> 01:30:14.910
Fine."

01:30:14.910 --> 01:30:18.520
And so I'd hang out and if I finish my drink
first, I'd be like, "Hey, does anyone else

01:30:18.520 --> 01:30:19.530
need a drink?"

01:30:19.530 --> 01:30:21.830
Don't be a fucking weirdo, just be a normal
person.

01:30:21.830 --> 01:30:24.420
But anyway, "Hey, does anyone else need a
drink?

01:30:24.420 --> 01:30:25.420
I'm going to grab another one."

01:30:25.420 --> 01:30:26.420
"Okay, great."

01:30:26.420 --> 01:30:27.420
Get a couple drinks.

01:30:27.420 --> 01:30:30.920
And eventually somebody in the group would
be like, "What's your story?

01:30:30.920 --> 01:30:31.920
Who are you?

01:30:31.920 --> 01:30:32.990
Why are you here?"

01:30:32.990 --> 01:30:37.220
And I would give some answer along the lines
of what I just mentioned.

01:30:37.220 --> 01:30:38.470
And then I would just stop.

01:30:38.470 --> 01:30:40.000
I would not start pitching.

01:30:40.000 --> 01:30:45.460
I would not immediately start humping their
legs to ask them if they wanted to read an

01:30:45.460 --> 01:30:47.430
advanced copy or something like that.

01:30:47.430 --> 01:30:53.400
But I would say that, and then I would leave
the window open to more questions, and then

01:30:53.400 --> 01:30:57.570
I would see if anyone took the bait.

01:30:57.570 --> 01:31:02.020
And if they did, that is an indicator of,
at least I took it as an indicator of interest.

01:31:02.020 --> 01:31:05.670
So let's say one of the people's like, "Well,
okay, so you said your book is — what's

01:31:05.670 --> 01:31:06.670
the book about?"

01:31:06.670 --> 01:31:08.060
And then I would elaborate.

01:31:08.060 --> 01:31:10.950
Then maybe they would ask some follow-up questions.

01:31:10.950 --> 01:31:14.870
And then at some point I would offer, I would
simply say, "Look, it might not be of any

01:31:14.870 --> 01:31:15.870
interest to you.

01:31:15.870 --> 01:31:18.080
I totally understand if you're just too busy.

01:31:18.080 --> 01:31:22.610
But there are I think maybe one or two chapters
that would be really interesting to you.

01:31:22.610 --> 01:31:25.170
If you'd like, I have a ton of advanced copies.

01:31:25.170 --> 01:31:31.390
I could just send you a copy and put in some
post-it notes for the 20 pages or so that

01:31:31.390 --> 01:31:33.920
I think would be most interesting."

01:31:33.920 --> 01:31:39.730
And I would say, I don't know, 60, 70 percent
of the time that I went up to one of those

01:31:39.730 --> 01:31:43.170
groups, at least one person would be like,
"Yeah, that sounds cool.

01:31:43.170 --> 01:31:44.170
Yeah, totally.

01:31:44.170 --> 01:31:45.170
Yeah, please send it."

01:31:45.170 --> 01:31:53.250
And that is a high-labor approach that also
can have a very, very high ROI.

01:31:53.250 --> 01:31:57.180
And I would replicate that.

01:31:57.180 --> 01:32:00.700
I still think in-person is the least crowded.

01:32:00.700 --> 01:32:06.700
It just takes a set of skills that most people
have neglected I think in a world where everything

01:32:06.700 --> 01:32:10.660
is thought to be digital and text first via
email.

01:32:10.660 --> 01:32:18.370
The interpersonal piece I think gets lost
a bit and the ability to navigate social dynamics

01:32:18.370 --> 01:32:26.311
in a way that is interesting, but not overwhelming
or irritating takes practice.

01:32:26.311 --> 01:32:27.960
You've got to practice this stuff.

01:32:27.960 --> 01:32:33.730
And fortunately, I got a lot of practice doing
that on the phone and in-person in my first

01:32:33.730 --> 01:32:43.540
job out of college, which was being an outside
sales rep for fiber channel storage area networks,

01:32:43.540 --> 01:32:47.090
I had to learn how to navigate this stuff.

01:32:47.090 --> 01:32:50.070
So in-person, it still works or it can work.

01:32:50.070 --> 01:32:56.010
All right, let me check out some more questions
in the live chat.

