I think it all that we'll do is draw attention
to it and increase interest in it.

01:33:17.990 --> 01:33:20.881
I don't see the downside and I certainly don't
have to share all of it.

01:33:20.881 --> 01:33:24.150
It could be, I don't know, 70 or 80 percent.

01:33:24.150 --> 01:33:25.150
Okay, here's a question.

01:33:25.150 --> 01:33:29.180
"What's your gut response to the metaverse
and its potential use cases?"

01:33:29.180 --> 01:33:30.180
Very conflicted.

01:33:30.180 --> 01:33:31.180
Very conflicted.

01:33:31.180 --> 01:33:43.490
I think as much as people talk about the utopia
and ideology of decentralization, humans like

01:33:43.490 --> 01:33:50.240
centralized discovery or some centralized
service where they do not need to make decisions.

01:33:50.240 --> 01:33:52.800
Humans are fucking lazy.

01:33:52.800 --> 01:33:56.320
And I'll throw myself in there too, honestly.

01:33:56.320 --> 01:34:04.800
Discovery is hard and humans want to identify
tastemakers or curators to help them with

01:34:04.800 --> 01:34:10.460
making choices in areas of high complexity
or in situations where there is a paradox

01:34:10.460 --> 01:34:12.840
of choice challenge, which is almost everywhere.

01:34:12.840 --> 01:34:21.590
And this will certainly be true in the metaverse
or the metaverses, although I find that oxymoronic,

01:34:21.590 --> 01:34:24.300
I guess, when we start looking at it that
way.

01:34:24.300 --> 01:34:36.580
So I do think that there will be likely a
handful at most of virtual spaces like the

01:34:36.580 --> 01:34:43.410
metaverse in Snow Crash, let's say, where
people interact through avatars.

01:34:43.410 --> 01:34:50.120
So let's just call it in a Second Life on
mega steroids with all sorts of technological

01:34:50.120 --> 01:34:55.650
innovations that have started to crest in
the last handful of years.

01:34:55.650 --> 01:34:59.490
I find it hard to believe that there are going
to be dozens and dozens and dozens of these

01:34:59.490 --> 01:35:01.330
things.

01:35:01.330 --> 01:35:10.550
I find it hard to believe that that will be
compelling to users if a few dominant players

01:35:10.550 --> 01:35:18.950
with massive amounts of capital and employee
coordination are focused on creating the most

01:35:18.950 --> 01:35:23.600
addictive compelling versions of these spaces.

01:35:23.600 --> 01:35:31.670
I hope I'm wrong, but if I've noticed anything
in the last year or two, it's that people

01:35:31.670 --> 01:35:35.850
claim to hate centralization, but man, do
they love it.

01:35:35.850 --> 01:35:39.090
It saves them a lot of time and it saves them
a lot of decisions.

01:35:39.090 --> 01:35:40.630
So we'll see where it goes.

01:35:40.630 --> 01:35:43.060
Oh yeah, okay.

