01:36:25.320 --> 01:36:30.790
We've got the biggest, check out the size
of these things, the biggest Swiss chard you

01:36:30.790 --> 01:36:31.790
can imagine.

01:36:31.790 --> 01:36:34.190
Look at this.

01:36:34.190 --> 01:36:35.830
That's Swiss chard.

01:36:35.830 --> 01:36:39.050
It's the size of my entire upper torso.

01:36:39.050 --> 01:36:46.190
So it did work this year, and I'd say the
two main differences are relocating the raised

01:36:46.190 --> 01:36:50.560
beds so they have more consistent sun exposure.

01:36:50.560 --> 01:36:57.990
But above and beyond that two things, setting
up a very, very simple electric fence that

01:36:57.990 --> 01:36:59.050
can be turned off and on.

01:36:59.050 --> 01:37:01.810
It's solar powered and it's very, very thin
meshed.

01:37:01.810 --> 01:37:08.420
It's just to keep out, to the extent possible,
the deer, the groundhog, all this kind of

01:37:08.420 --> 01:37:09.420
stuff.

01:37:09.420 --> 01:37:16.720
And then second is an aeration system, a timed
watering system.

01:37:16.720 --> 01:37:20.430
So simple, not that difficult to install.

01:37:20.430 --> 01:37:27.270
And the combination of all those factors has
led to just an incredible harvest this year,

01:37:27.270 --> 01:37:32.480
for the first time ever in my life, tons and
tons of stuff.

01:37:32.480 --> 01:37:35.880
For whatever reason, the kale gets annihilated
by pests.

01:37:35.880 --> 01:37:38.940
Everything else is fine and intact, the kale
gets completely killed.

01:37:38.940 --> 01:37:45.840
I'm not using any pesticides, but for some
reason, the kale specifically is just the

01:37:45.840 --> 01:37:47.000
sacrificial lamb.

01:37:47.000 --> 01:37:56.050
It seems to get absolutely naced by everything
that wants to snack on some domesticated veggies.

01:37:56.050 --> 01:37:57.050
So there you have it, but thanks for asking.

01:37:57.050 --> 01:37:58.970
I can feel my brain slowing down a little
bit, guys, so I'm probably going to do another

01:37:58.970 --> 01:37:59.970
10 to 15 minutes.

01:37:59.970 --> 01:38:00.970
All right.

01:38:00.970 --> 01:38:01.970
Ranjit: "What is your workout routine these
days?"

01:38:01.970 --> 01:38:03.630
My workout routine's super simple.

01:38:03.630 --> 01:38:09.270
I go to the gym and I lift weights doing full-body
workouts, and it's called twice a week.

01:38:09.270 --> 01:38:11.040
That's weight training.

01:38:11.040 --> 01:38:19.020
I have a sled, a weighted sled that I push,
I would say three to four times per week in

01:38:19.020 --> 01:38:22.240
the morning, usually after my first cold brew.

01:38:22.240 --> 01:38:26.300
And I am intermittent fasting at the moment
where I will effectively not have anything

01:38:26.300 --> 01:38:28.120
to eat until noon.

01:38:28.120 --> 01:38:32.240
I say effectively because sometimes I will
have a few macadamia nuts or something like

01:38:32.240 --> 01:38:33.240
that.

01:38:33.240 --> 01:38:37.700
But generally no calories to very, very low
calories.

01:38:37.700 --> 01:38:44.520
So I'm intermittent fasting in the morning
and am doing kettlebell swings.

01:38:44.520 --> 01:38:52.080
Right now I think I'm using a 72-pounder for
sets of 50 and really just doing one set of

01:38:52.080 --> 01:38:56.470
50 to a hundred reps twice a week.

01:38:56.470 --> 01:39:03.570
And I jump rope in the mornings oftentimes
before I do the sled pushing just as a way

01:39:03.570 --> 01:39:10.010
to wake up my nervous system and my cognition,
honestly, just jumping rope for two minutes,

01:39:10.010 --> 01:39:15.400
hopping around, having my dog bark her face
off at me, which is hilarious, is enough to

01:39:15.400 --> 01:39:18.270
change my mood, to elevate mood.

01:39:18.270 --> 01:39:25.450
Sauna, probably four to five times a week,
generally before dinner.

01:39:25.450 --> 01:39:27.470
So let's just say I'm eating at seven o'clock.

01:39:27.470 --> 01:39:35.290
I will finish up my day, my work day at five
or 5:30, do the sauna for a half hour, and

01:39:35.290 --> 01:39:36.950
then take a cold shower post.

01:39:36.950 --> 01:39:38.350
That's about it.

01:39:38.350 --> 01:39:39.750
That's about it.

01:39:39.750 --> 01:39:41.150
It's nothing crazy.

01:39:41.150 --> 01:39:46.050
If I go for a hike with my dog, which I do
pretty regularly, if the weather permits,

01:39:46.050 --> 01:39:47.320
I will throw on a rucksack.

01:39:47.320 --> 01:39:51.660
I have a GORUCKsack for an hour hike.

01:39:51.660 --> 01:39:56.540
I'm not using crazy weight also because this
rucksack does not have a waist strap.

01:39:56.540 --> 01:40:04.880
So I am supporting all the weight on my traps
and shoulders usually use 35 pound weight.

01:40:04.880 --> 01:40:10.280
But you'll notice none of this takes more
than — the weight training at the gym is

01:40:10.280 --> 01:40:12.300
going to take the most time because of the
transit.

01:40:12.300 --> 01:40:17.220
There are weeks when I just skip that all
together and I'll do the other things I described,

01:40:17.220 --> 01:40:23.920
but we're talking about at most 30 minutes
a day, unless it's an activity that doesn't

01:40:23.920 --> 01:40:28.610
function for me exclusively as exercise, like
an hour long hike with my dog, then I'm just

01:40:28.610 --> 01:40:31.570
throwing on weights to get some additional
stimulation.

01:40:31.570 --> 01:40:34.280
But that's what the workout looks like these
days.

01:40:34.280 --> 01:40:37.280
I am using a new device that I've enjoyed.

01:40:37.280 --> 01:40:40.400
I'll just go grab them to show what they look
like.

01:40:40.400 --> 01:40:46.590
Okay, so for decreasing lower back pain or
alleviating lower back pain and also helping

01:40:46.590 --> 01:40:51.400
tremendously with sleep, there are two devices
that I've been using for some time now.

01:40:51.400 --> 01:40:58.790
This one is called the Pso-Rite, and you lay
on top of this, and for those who can't see

01:40:58.790 --> 01:41:07.650
this, it is a piece of plastic that looks
almost like a U. And the tops of either side

01:41:07.650 --> 01:41:16.510
of the U are these rounded plastic ridges
that you lay on top of and they apply pressure

01:41:16.510 --> 01:41:17.551
to your psoas.

01:41:17.551 --> 01:41:24.340
So this is called a Pso-Rite device, pso-rite.com.

01:41:24.340 --> 01:41:29.670
And this is a device that I used last night,
for instance, prior to sleep.

01:41:29.670 --> 01:41:36.290
And I've been comparing and contrasting that
with another option, which is a lot easier

01:41:36.290 --> 01:41:37.610
to travel with.

01:41:37.610 --> 01:41:41.400
So I have traveled, for instance, when I was
hiking in the mountains and at altitude, I

01:41:41.400 --> 01:41:42.400
traveled with this.

01:41:42.400 --> 01:41:51.300
So if you look at this thing here, this is
the Hip Hook and it more effectively gets

01:41:51.300 --> 01:41:56.290
into, and you can see how aggressive this
looks, right?

01:41:56.290 --> 01:41:58.280
You're laying on this.

01:41:58.280 --> 01:42:05.010
So this really gets into your psoas and can
also get to the iliacus more effectively than

01:42:05.010 --> 01:42:06.010
the Pso-Rite.

01:42:06.010 --> 01:42:14.041
I'm not an anatomist, but if you think of
where your psoas is, let's just say a few

01:42:14.041 --> 01:42:20.060
inches up from your iliac crest, those hip
bones that stick out right under your obliques

01:42:20.060 --> 01:42:26.350
or love handles depending on your condition,
those hips, if you go directly up your sides

01:42:26.350 --> 01:42:32.870
and then towards the center, say, I don't
know, three or four inches, and then press

01:42:32.870 --> 01:42:39.460
directly towards your spine, this is not a
precise description, but you will be getting

01:42:39.460 --> 01:42:42.440
into your psoas.

01:42:42.440 --> 01:42:43.440
The iliopsoas.

01:42:43.440 --> 01:42:51.880
The iliacus is going to be lower down, inside
the cradle of the hip architecture, which

01:42:51.880 --> 01:42:53.850
is where the Hip Hook comes in.

01:42:53.850 --> 01:42:59.950
And this really works well, I've been using
it for the last several weeks on the road.

01:42:59.950 --> 01:43:06.020
And these two I have found to be tremendously
helpful for improving sleep.

01:43:06.020 --> 01:43:10.430
Often what disrupts my sleep is lower back
tightness and I end up having to flip flop

01:43:10.430 --> 01:43:15.240
like a rotisserie chicken from side to side
to side to side, which, surprise, surprise,

01:43:15.240 --> 01:43:19.450
does not improve your sleep architecture or
quality.

01:43:19.450 --> 01:43:23.130
So these two devices I have found very helpful.

01:43:23.130 --> 01:43:29.080
Let me do another question here from Michael.

01:43:29.080 --> 01:43:40.050
"What would be your suggested criteria for
choosing podcast to allocate advertising dollars

01:43:40.050 --> 01:43:44.940
towards product/industry agnostic if possible?"

01:43:44.940 --> 01:43:46.370
This is a difficult question to answer.

01:43:46.370 --> 01:43:51.130
I would say because there are a number of
articles that have been written about it.

01:43:51.130 --> 01:44:01.550
Athletic Greens has the most methodical, quantitative
and strategic approach to podcast advertising

01:44:01.550 --> 01:44:03.820
that I have ever seen in any company.

01:44:03.820 --> 01:44:09.780
So to the extent that you can study that,
and there are articles that discuss their

01:44:09.780 --> 01:44:14.360
approach to podcast advertising, I think it
is it worth looking at.

01:44:14.360 --> 01:44:22.560
It is not possible, I think, to make any answer
product/industry agnostic because Athletic

01:44:22.560 --> 01:44:31.900
Greens has a high-end premium product with
a very high LTV, customer lifetime value.

01:44:31.900 --> 01:44:35.420
And you can timebound that, let's just say
their value over two years or three years,

01:44:35.420 --> 01:44:36.610
something like that.

01:44:36.610 --> 01:44:41.070
But it is a product that has a very low amount
of churn.

01:44:41.070 --> 01:44:46.560
In other words, once someone subscribes to
receive Athletic Greens, they tend to remain

01:44:46.560 --> 01:44:50.080
an Athletic Greens user.

01:44:50.080 --> 01:44:57.470
And this is the type of product that I think
is particularly well-suited to certain types

01:44:57.470 --> 01:45:05.380
of podcasts, especially long form interview
podcasts where ad reads are host-read.

01:45:05.380 --> 01:45:09.550
Very specific I know, but Athletic Greens
is worth studying.

01:45:09.550 --> 01:45:15.180
And then an additional factor that is less
discussed.

01:45:15.180 --> 01:45:22.150
Because on the buy side, if you are a podcast
advertiser, generally you don't want to allocate,

01:45:22.150 --> 01:45:26.910
you don't want to discuss the implicit value
of what I'm going to describe because it lessens

01:45:26.910 --> 01:45:28.510
your bargaining position.

01:45:28.510 --> 01:45:34.660
If you're on the buy side for the podcast
equation, you want to say, "Okay, here is

01:45:34.660 --> 01:45:38.840
our URL with a code at the end," and people
are going to go to this link.

01:45:38.840 --> 01:45:41.490
We're going to track how much traffic goes
to that link.

01:45:41.490 --> 01:45:44.260
And then they need to enter this really onerous
code.

01:45:44.260 --> 01:45:54.930
It's TIM08#43_7, and based on how many people
redeem that code, we will see what the ROI

01:45:54.930 --> 01:45:57.690
is on our podcasting advertisement.

01:45:57.690 --> 01:46:02.140
Now anyone listening to the exaggerated example
I just gave will realize there's going to

01:46:02.140 --> 01:46:05.050
be a tremendous amount of slippage.

01:46:05.050 --> 01:46:06.080
What does this mean?

01:46:06.080 --> 01:46:09.451
This means that the vast majority of people
aren't going to remember, nor even if they

01:46:09.451 --> 01:46:16.050
remember take the time to input such a code
if the savings is for instance, say 10 percent,

01:46:16.050 --> 01:46:22.160
which is why I almost always insist that if
sponsors are going to give a discount, they

01:46:22.160 --> 01:46:25.530
make it compelling enough so that people will
use an actual tracking code.

01:46:25.530 --> 01:46:32.770
Because if they are setting the measure of
success based on a code, which most people

01:46:32.770 --> 01:46:42.500
will not put in, unless the discount is significant
30 to 50 percent, then I am being set up for

01:46:42.500 --> 01:46:43.500
failure.

01:46:43.500 --> 01:46:48.830
Because, especially this is true with larger
companies, if there is someone who's a middle

01:46:48.830 --> 01:46:56.840
manager, junior VP of marketing, or associate
director of marketing who is in charge of

01:46:56.840 --> 01:47:04.920
monitoring ROI and they're basing everything
on, say, the one out of 10 people I refer

01:47:04.920 --> 01:47:12.610
who actually fill in a code, then they are
deliberately or unknowingly putting themselves

01:47:12.610 --> 01:47:16.280
in a position to say the podcast ad didn't
work.

01:47:16.280 --> 01:47:19.550
This fortunately doesn't happen very often
with me, but I'm painting a picture that is

01:47:19.550 --> 01:47:24.060
something that applies all over the industry.

01:47:24.060 --> 01:47:27.370
So there are a couple of things here to keep
in mind, right?

01:47:27.370 --> 01:47:30.530
There's slippage number one, you're not going
to track anything perfectly.

01:47:30.530 --> 01:47:34.920
Unless it goes through something like a newsletter,
in which case, now I have access to the data

01:47:34.920 --> 01:47:35.920
as well.

01:47:35.920 --> 01:47:40.320
I can look at the click-through rates and
I can identify, for instance, is it a copy

01:47:40.320 --> 01:47:41.330
issue?

01:47:41.330 --> 01:47:42.440
Is it a read issue?

01:47:42.440 --> 01:47:47.740
Or, and this is very common, is it a website
conversion issue?

01:47:47.740 --> 01:47:52.050
My team spends quite a bit of time with podcast
advertisers looking at their websites saying,

01:47:52.050 --> 01:47:56.980
"Hey, look, we can send a bunch of fish towards
your net, but your net sucks.

01:47:56.980 --> 01:47:58.260
It's full of holes right now.

01:47:58.260 --> 01:47:59.550
Doesn't make any sense.

01:47:59.550 --> 01:48:06.400
It's upside down and we're not going to do
it unless your conversion can be optimized."

01:48:06.400 --> 01:48:14.000
We're more polite about it than that, but
it's critical that I think for me at least,

01:48:14.000 --> 01:48:21.200
because I care about having long term sponsors
for extended periods of time, it's a hassle

01:48:21.200 --> 01:48:27.840
and very energy intensive for my team to deal
with any type of churn that I want, all pieces

01:48:27.840 --> 01:48:35.690
of the chain to function optimally before
I put forth the effort to test all these products

01:48:35.690 --> 01:48:40.130
and services and then vet them down to the
top five percent that make the cut.

01:48:40.130 --> 01:48:41.130
And then to do a good read.

01:48:41.130 --> 01:48:44.690
I don't want to go through all that hassle
if someone's not going to do the heavy lifting

01:48:44.690 --> 01:48:46.630
on their side to ensure optimal conversion.

01:48:46.630 --> 01:48:52.050
I know we're getting a little off topic, but
the reason that I bring all of this up is

01:48:52.050 --> 01:48:53.920
allocating advertising dollars.

01:48:53.920 --> 01:48:56.280
How should you allocate advertising dollars?

01:48:56.280 --> 01:49:00.150
There are different reasons you may advertise,
and I'll give an example.

01:49:00.150 --> 01:49:07.140
There was a billboard in the Bay Area, it
was on 280, which takes people from the airport,

01:49:07.140 --> 01:49:12.920
from SFO to San Francisco, and there's this
big billboard and it was a huge tech company.

01:49:12.920 --> 01:49:18.280
I'm not going to mention the name because
I don't know if they'd like it, but the advertisement

01:49:18.280 --> 01:49:27.940
didn't really make any sense to anyone, except
for one tiny sliver of the population.

01:49:27.940 --> 01:49:30.780
Huge billboard, no doubt, very expensive.

01:49:30.780 --> 01:49:33.410
And lots of people drove by it and they're
like, "That's the dumbest billboard ever.

01:49:33.410 --> 01:49:34.920
It doesn't even make any sense."

01:49:34.920 --> 01:49:40.270
And then someone in this group conversation
said, "Actually, it makes perfect sense.

01:49:40.270 --> 01:49:43.030
You just have to realize who that billboard
is for.

01:49:43.030 --> 01:49:46.490
It is for investment bankers who are visiting
the Bay Area.

01:49:46.490 --> 01:49:48.570
That's it."

01:49:48.570 --> 01:49:53.630
So if you're looking at that billboard for
conversion purposes and there's some unique

01:49:53.630 --> 01:49:59.360
URL, the value is probably going to amount
to zero.

01:49:59.360 --> 01:50:06.400
If you are looking at it from a customer acquisition
perspective, and this was a gaming company,

01:50:06.400 --> 01:50:10.150
the value is probably zero if you're viewing
it.

01:50:10.150 --> 01:50:17.440
However, through the lens of courting the
top operators in the rarefied world of investment

01:50:17.440 --> 01:50:25.440
banking, okay now perhaps you only need one
person or two people or five people to pay

01:50:25.440 --> 01:50:31.130
attention to that, and suddenly that can be
worth many millions upon millions of dollars

01:50:31.130 --> 01:50:33.040
for a given company.

01:50:33.040 --> 01:50:38.340
The reason I bring that up is you should ask
yourself, who do I want to know this brand?

01:50:38.340 --> 01:50:43.340
Who do I want to understand the marketing
positioning of this brand?

01:50:43.340 --> 01:50:51.780
Is it for instance, the broadest population
possible, say all readers of USA Today and

01:50:51.780 --> 01:50:59.030
all viewers of late night television, something
like that?

01:50:59.030 --> 01:51:04.900
Or is it most valuable for me to get in front
of, say, the audience of TED?

01:51:04.900 --> 01:51:11.100
Would I want just the two to 3,000 people
at TED to all be aware of this?

01:51:11.100 --> 01:51:14.140
And that can dictate your choice of platform.

01:51:14.140 --> 01:51:19.730
So if you want to say, get in front of New
York Times, Wall Street Journal, policymakers,

01:51:19.730 --> 01:51:25.570
tastemakers, directors, producers, et cetera,
I know for a fact that you can very easily

01:51:25.570 --> 01:51:31.830
do that through the podcast, my podcast that
is, or through 5-Bullet Friday, through the

01:51:31.830 --> 01:51:32.830
newsletter, right?

01:51:32.830 --> 01:51:35.200
And that is highly, highly, highly targeted.

01:51:35.200 --> 01:51:39.420
The absolute numbers may not be large, even
though the newsletter's got two million subscribers

01:51:39.420 --> 01:51:40.420
or so.

01:51:40.420 --> 01:51:45.330
In the grand scheme of things, in a country
of whatever, the 350 to 400 million people,

01:51:45.330 --> 01:51:51.590
that's not a gigantic percentage, but you
don't need a gigantic percentage, if similar

01:51:51.590 --> 01:51:54.910
to our earlier conversations, you choose the
right lead dominoes.

01:51:54.910 --> 01:52:02.310
And this maps perfectly to how I launched
The 4-Hour Workweek also, in choosing outlets.

01:52:02.310 --> 01:52:03.680
How did I choose outlets?

01:52:03.680 --> 01:52:11.280
I was looking at the first domino as the very
tightly defined demographic of say, 20- to

01:52:11.280 --> 01:52:19.980
35-year-old tech savvy males living in New
York, San Francisco, and Los Angeles.

01:52:19.980 --> 01:52:20.980
That was it.

01:52:20.980 --> 01:52:27.830
And what that allowed me to do as someone
with very little budget was to take whatever

01:52:27.830 --> 01:52:34.350
time and capital I had and to think about
four or five outlets, let's just say at the

01:52:34.350 --> 01:52:41.990
time, probably Gizmodo, TechCrunch, Lifehacker
perhaps, and a few others.

01:52:41.990 --> 01:52:48.260
And Robert Scoble was a very, very influential
blogger at the time, if I could get noticed

01:52:48.260 --> 01:52:53.960
and some degree of coverage from these outlets,
I would create the perception of surround

01:52:53.960 --> 01:53:00.400
sound in so much as I would appear with The
4-Hour Workweek to be ubiquitous to a very,

01:53:00.400 --> 01:53:08.000
very tightly defined demographic, recognizing
that the target, the initial target market

01:53:08.000 --> 01:53:09.590
is not the total market.

01:53:09.590 --> 01:53:12.660
And people get all caught up about this where
they're like, "Oh God.

01:53:12.660 --> 01:53:15.150
Well, what if we know we need to be inclusive
with everybody?"

01:53:15.150 --> 01:53:19.120
If you try to make everyone your customer,
no one is going to be your customer.

01:53:19.120 --> 01:53:22.130
You will fail 99 times out of a hundred.

01:53:22.130 --> 01:53:24.980
There is a time to broaden, but it is not
in the early phases.

01:53:24.980 --> 01:53:28.740
In the beginning, you need the strongest foothold
and handhold possible.

01:53:28.740 --> 01:53:31.820
So you need to choose your niche.

01:53:31.820 --> 01:53:36.040
And this is true with so many products that
end up being mainstream successes.

01:53:36.040 --> 01:53:39.330
They do not begin by trying to boil the ocean
at once.

01:53:39.330 --> 01:53:45.630
They choose a very surgical, precise demographic.

01:53:45.630 --> 01:53:51.590
So I started with that, and the expectation
was if I were able to establish a critical

01:53:51.590 --> 01:53:56.940
mass of attention within that group, that
it would then cascade.

01:53:56.940 --> 01:53:58.880
It would cascade from male to female.

01:53:58.880 --> 01:54:03.860
Now you have 20- to 35-year-old tech-savvy
females in the same cities looking at the

01:54:03.860 --> 01:54:06.010
same outlets, and that is exactly what happened.

01:54:06.010 --> 01:54:13.260
And then from there, the age would start to
bleed out and creep both younger and older.

01:54:13.260 --> 01:54:14.570
That's exactly what happened.

01:54:14.570 --> 01:54:21.030
And then from there, from those cities with
very high density and large populations, including

01:54:21.030 --> 01:54:27.800
a critical mass of people with broadcast capabilities,
let's say bloggers, people using Twitter,

01:54:27.800 --> 01:54:32.990
et cetera, which launched publicly officially
at the same South by Southwest that The 4-Hour

01:54:32.990 --> 01:54:42.340
Workweek did in March of 2007, that if you
choose the right initial dominoes, you can

01:54:42.340 --> 01:54:47.180
allow cascades to take care of a lot of the
heavy lifting later.

01:54:47.180 --> 01:54:50.090
But if you are imprecise in the beginning,
you're fucked.

01:54:50.090 --> 01:54:51.280
It just will not work.

01:54:51.280 --> 01:54:56.920
Even if you, I had a million dollars to put
into The 4-Hour Workweek launch, and I think

01:54:56.920 --> 01:55:05.030
I capped my total expenses at $10 or $20 grand,
almost all of which went to events registration

01:55:05.030 --> 01:55:08.570
and travel and housing for those events, just
FYI.

01:55:08.570 --> 01:55:15.840
But if instead of that 20 grand I'd had a
million dollars, number one, I would've made

01:55:15.840 --> 01:55:19.320
much worse decisions, I would've been much
less precise.

01:55:19.320 --> 01:55:21.570
I would've been much less creative.

01:55:21.570 --> 01:55:26.000
And if I had made poor decisions in the beginning,
chosen the wrong dominoes, and then tried

01:55:26.000 --> 01:55:33.940
to fix it, say six months later by pouring
money into it would've all been cash in a

01:55:33.940 --> 01:55:36.340
pile lit on fire on the sidewalk.

01:55:36.340 --> 01:55:37.690
It wouldn't have done anything.

01:55:37.690 --> 01:55:40.530
So how the hell did we get onto all this?

01:55:40.530 --> 01:55:43.070
Wow, I've got a preacher's fire my belly.

01:55:43.070 --> 01:55:44.070
I don't know what happened there.

01:55:44.070 --> 01:55:47.940
Oh yes, we were talking about suggested criteria
for choosing podcast allocate advertising

01:55:47.940 --> 01:55:48.940
dollars towards.

01:55:48.940 --> 01:55:49.940
All right, so there you go.

01:55:49.940 --> 01:55:56.420
You got a long rambling sermon instead of
a short answer, but hopefully that at least

01:55:56.420 --> 01:56:04.890
provides some questions that might be helpful
and a recognition of some of the invisible

01:56:04.890 --> 01:56:13.050
risks and benefits of choosing through one
criterion alone.

01:56:13.050 --> 01:56:20.450
If you run a direct-to-consume or direct-response
business, you need at least some, if not most

01:56:20.450 --> 01:56:26.610
of your advertising to return a positive ROI
or a media efficiency ratio.

01:56:26.610 --> 01:56:28.260
But you don't need all of it to do that.

01:56:28.260 --> 01:56:29.460
You don't need all of it to do that.

01:56:29.460 --> 01:56:36.770
There is some degree of brand advertising
or creation of awareness, which unfortunately

01:56:36.770 --> 01:56:43.410
are smoke screen excuses that get used to
justify a lot of decision making.

01:56:43.410 --> 01:56:47.660
But you can be very surgical about how you
might approach such things.

01:56:47.660 --> 01:56:53.490
And in the very beginning, for instance, sponsoring
the UFC for my sports nutrition company was

01:56:53.490 --> 01:56:54.490
exactly that.

01:56:54.490 --> 01:56:59.420
I did not need the UFC to return all of my
sponsorship cost.

01:56:59.420 --> 01:57:04.880
It was a strategic decision on a whole bunch
of different levels, and you can afford a

01:57:04.880 --> 01:57:12.280
couple of those experiments as long as you
recognize or assume that the trackable ROI

01:57:12.280 --> 01:57:16.110
will likely be $0, at least in the intermediate
phase.

